"""Form 5 Geology (O Level 0555): modules 4 to 6 and the Examination module (18 lessons).

Draft content for teacher review. Exam facts are only those of the Board's 2023 report: Paper 1 is 50 multiple-choice
questions, all answered; Paper 2 has seven structural questions, five answered (two from Section A, two from Section B
and the compulsory school-based assessment question 7). No mark splits are stated. Cameroon facts (Lake Monoun 1984,
Lake Nyos 1986, Rio del Rey and Douala basins, bauxite, gold, iron ore, rutile, limestone) are general and hedged with
'check your textbook'; no production statistics are given. Numerical examples (half-life, true thickness, porosity,
discharge, scale, S-P lag, tenor) were verified by script. Teaching figures are labelled as invented for practice.
"""

from .dsl import *  # noqa: F403

COURSE = ("geology", "form-5")

M4 = "Geomorphology & Surface Processes"
M5 = "Structural Geology, Stratigraphy & Paleontology"
M6 = "Hydrogeology, Economic & Environmental Geology"
M7 = "Examination"

LESSONS = [
    lesson(
        module=M4, topic="Weathering",
        title="Weathering: physical, chemical and biological",
        slug="gly2-weathering-physical-chemical-biological",
        minutes=22,
        summary="Weathering breaks rock down where it lies. Physical weathering splits it, chemical weathering changes its minerals (hydration, carbonation, oxidation, hydrolysis, solution) and living things help both. Heat and rain speed it up.",
        objectives=[
            "Define weathering and separate it from erosion",
            "Describe physical, chemical and biological weathering with an example of each",
            "Define hydration, carbonation and oxidation correctly and write the limestone reaction in words and symbols",
            "Explain why weathering is fast in the warm, wet climate of southern Cameroon",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Weathering", "Erosion", "Physical weathering", "Chemical weathering", "Hydration", "Carbonation", "Oxidation", "Biological weathering"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("Look at an old concrete wall or a granite boulder beside a road near Buea or Bafoussam. The surface is rough, stained and crumbling, and small pieces lie at its foot. Nobody carried those pieces away. The rock simply broke down where it stood. Geologists call this **weathering**, and it is the first step in making soil, sediment and many landscapes."),
            D("**Weathering** is the breakdown of rocks into smaller pieces or new minerals **in the same place**, at or near the Earth's surface. **Erosion** is different: it is the removal and carrying away of weathered material by water, wind, ice or gravity.", title="Weathering and erosion"),
            KEY("Three kinds of weathering", "Weathering is grouped by what does the work.", cards=[
                ["Physical (mechanical)", "The rock is split into pieces. Its minerals do not change."],
                ["Chemical", "The minerals react with water, gases or acids and change into new substances."],
                ["Biological", "Plants, animals and people help the rock to break, physically or chemically."]]),
            H("Physical weathering"),
            T("In **thermal expansion**, a rock is heated by day and cooled at night. Minerals expand and contract by different amounts, and stresses build until the surface layers crack and peel off like the skin of an onion. This peeling is called **exfoliation**. In **frost wedging**, water freezes in cracks and expands, forcing them wider. This happens in cold places and on very high mountains, not in lowland Cameroon. **Salt crystal growth** works in a similar way: salty water enters pores, dries, and the growing crystals push grains apart. **Pressure release** happens when erosion removes the rock above a deep body such as granite. The rock expands upwards and splits into curved sheets parallel to the surface."),
            IMG("f5gly2-cycle.svg", alt="Diagram of the hydrologic cycle showing evaporation, condensation, precipitation, runoff, infiltration and groundwater flow", caption="Figure 1. Water drives most chemical weathering. Rain that soaks into the ground (infiltration) reacts with the minerals."),
            H("Chemical weathering"),
            D("**Hydration** is the addition of water to a mineral so that it changes in chemical composition. **Carbonation** is the reaction of carbon dioxide and water, which form weak carbonic acid, with minerals in rocks, breaking them down. **Oxidation** is the reaction of oxygen with minerals in rocks to form oxides.", title="Three processes examiners ask for"),
            T("Two more processes are in most textbooks. **Hydrolysis** is the reaction of minerals such as feldspar with water, producing clay minerals and dissolved ions. **Solution** is the simple dissolving of soluble minerals such as halite (rock salt) in water. Check your textbook for the exact wording used in your class."),
            F(r"CaCO_3 + H_2O + CO_2 \rightarrow Ca(HCO_3)_2", title="Carbonation of limestone"),
            T("Limestone is calcium carbonate. Rain absorbs carbon dioxide from the air and soil, so it is slightly acidic. The acid changes the solid calcium carbonate into calcium hydrogencarbonate, which is soluble and is carried away in water. That is how caves and sinkholes form in limestone country. In oxidation, iron in minerals such as biotite and pyrite combines with oxygen. The rock turns red or brown, like rust on a nail, and becomes weak."),
            H("Biological weathering"),
            T("Tree roots grow into cracks and widen them as they thicken. Burrowing animals such as termites and rodents expose fresh rock to air and water. Lichens and mosses release organic acids that attack minerals. Rotting plants make the soil water more acidic. People also weather rocks by quarrying, farming and building."),
            H("What controls the rate?"),
            KEY("Rate of weathering", "Four things matter most.", cards=[
                ["Climate", "Chemical weathering is fastest where it is hot and wet, such as the forest zone of southern Cameroon. Frost wedging needs freezing."],
                ["Rock type", "Quartz resists chemical attack. Feldspar and calcite break down more easily."],
                ["Cracks (joints)", "Joints let water and roots go deep, so more rock surface is attacked."],
                ["Time", "Weathering is slow, but small effects add up over thousands of years."]]),
            EX("Worked example 1: surface area", intro="A cube of rock has sides of 1 cm. Weathering splits it into 1 000 equal small cubes with sides of 0.1 cm. By what factor does the total surface area increase, and why does this help chemical weathering?", steps=[
                S("Area of the original cube", text="6 faces of 1 cm by 1 cm: 6 cm squared."),
                S("Area of one small cube", text="6 faces of 0.1 cm by 0.1 cm: 6 times 0.01 = 0.06 cm squared."),
                S("Total area of 1 000 small cubes", text="1 000 times 0.06 = 60 cm squared."),
                S("Compare and explain", text="60 divided by 6 = 10, so the area is ten times larger. More surface means more rock in contact with water and air, so physical weathering speeds up chemical weathering."),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: name the process", intro="Name the main weathering process in each case. (a) A red-brown crust forms on an iron-rich rock. (b) Sheets of granite peel off a bare hill after hot days and cool nights. (c) A fig root splits a concrete slab.", steps=[
                S("(a) Oxygen forms iron oxides", text="This is oxidation (chemical weathering)."),
                S("(b) Heating and cooling", text="This is thermal expansion with exfoliation (physical weathering)."),
                S("(c) A living root", text="This is biological weathering (root wedging)."),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Mixing up weathering and erosion", "Weathering breaks the rock where it is. Erosion carries pieces away. If the question says 'transported', the answer is erosion."),
            MISTAKE("Defining carbonation as 'adding carbon'", "Carbonation is the action of **carbonic acid**, made from carbon dioxide and water, on minerals. Say 'weak acid' in your definition."),
            QUIZ("Which process is the addition of water to a mineral so that its composition changes?", ["Oxidation", "Hydration", "Carbonation", "Exfoliation"], 1, "Hydration means water is added to the mineral.", hint="The name begins with the same letters as 'hydro', water.", feedback=["Oxidation uses oxygen.", "", "Carbonation uses carbonic acid from carbon dioxide and water.", "Exfoliation is peeling of rock sheets, a physical process."]),
            QUIZ("Why is chemical weathering so fast in the rainforest of southern Cameroon?", ["The rocks there are always frozen", "It is warm and wet, and rotting plants make the soil water acidic", "There are no joints in the rocks", "Wind carries all the weathered rock away at once"], 1, "Heat speeds reactions, rain supplies water, and plant acids attack minerals.", hint="Think of both temperature and water.", feedback=["There is no freezing in the lowlands.", "", "Joints do exist and help weathering.", "Wind transport is erosion, not the reason for fast weathering."]),
            TIP("Weathering appears in Paper 1 multiple choice and in structured questions. The Board's examiners reported that many candidates could not explain hydration, carbonation and oxidation, so practise the three definitions until you can write them from memory.", title="Exam link"),
            PQ("Define weathering and state two differences between physical and chemical weathering.", "Weathering is the breakdown of rock in place at or near the surface. In physical weathering the rock breaks into pieces without any change in its minerals, for example by thermal expansion. In chemical weathering the minerals change into new substances by reacting with water, gases or acids, for example by carbonation."),
            PQ("Write the word and symbol equation for the carbonation of limestone and say why the product is removed from the rock.", "Calcium carbonate + water + carbon dioxide gives calcium hydrogencarbonate: CaCO3 + H2O + CO2 gives Ca(HCO3)2. The product is soluble, so water carries it away and the limestone slowly dissolves."),
            PQ("Describe three ways in which living things cause weathering.", "Roots grow into joints and widen them. Burrowing animals expose fresh rock to air and water. Lichens and decaying plants produce organic acids that attack minerals."),
            SUM("Weathering is breakdown in place; erosion is removal.",
                "Physical weathering splits rock; chemical weathering changes the minerals; biological weathering helps both.",
                "Hydration adds water, carbonation uses carbonic acid, oxidation uses oxygen.",
                "Heat, rain, rock type and cracks control the rate; weathering is fast in hot, wet southern Cameroon."),
        ],
    ),
    lesson(
        module=M4, topic="Soil formation",
        title="Soil formation and the soil profile",
        slug="gly2-soil-formation-profile",
        minutes=20,
        summary="Soil is weathered rock mixed with humus, water and air. It forms in layers called horizons (O, A, B, C, bedrock) under the control of parent rock, climate, relief, living things and time.",
        objectives=[
            "Define soil and name its four components",
            "Describe the O, A, B and C horizons and bedrock in a soil profile",
            "List the five factors of soil formation",
            "Explain leaching and why many tropical soils are red and low in nutrients",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Soil", "Humus", "Soil profile", "Horizon", "Parent material", "Leaching", "Laterite"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("A farmer near Bamenda digs a hole for a new latrine. The top is dark and crumbly. Lower down the soil is redder and stickier. Deeper still there are broken stones, and then solid rock. The farmer has just seen a **soil profile**: a vertical slice through the soil showing how it changes with depth."),
            D("**Soil** is the loose, natural layer at the Earth's surface in which plants grow. It is made of **weathered rock particles**, **humus** (decayed plant and animal matter), **water** and **air**.", title="Soil"),
            T("Soil forms when weathering breaks a **parent material** (the rock or sediment underneath) into particles, and living things add humus. Plants then grow, die and add more humus. This takes hundreds to thousands of years."),
            KEY("Factors of soil formation", "Five factors decide what kind of soil forms.", cards=[
                ["Parent material", "Sandstone gives sandy soil; shale gives clayey soil; volcanic rock gives mineral-rich soil."],
                ["Climate", "Heat and rain control the speed of weathering and of leaching."],
                ["Relief", "On steep slopes soil is thin because it is eroded. On flat ground it is thicker."],
                ["Organisms", "Plants, bacteria, fungi and termites add humus and mix the soil."],
                ["Time", "Old soils are thicker and have clearer layers than young ones."]]),
            H("The soil profile"),
            IMG("f5gly2-soil.svg", alt="A soil profile with layers O, A, B, C and R from top to bottom and a short description beside each layer", caption="Figure 1. A soil profile. The layers are called horizons."),
            D("A **horizon** is a layer of soil that differs from the layers above and below it in colour, texture or composition. The letters O, A, B, C and R are used for the main ones.", title="Horizon"),
            KEY("The main horizons", "From the surface downwards.", cards=[
                ["O horizon", "Leaf litter and humus on the surface. Dark and rich in organic matter."],
                ["A horizon (topsoil)", "Dark mixture of humus and mineral grains. Plant roots are most common here. Water washes some fine particles and dissolved minerals out of it."],
                ["B horizon (subsoil)", "Lighter or redder. Clay, iron compounds and other washed-in materials collect here."],
                ["C horizon", "Partly weathered parent rock fragments, with little humus."],
                ["R: bedrock", "Solid, unweathered parent rock."]]),
            H("Leaching and tropical soils"),
            D("**Leaching** is the washing of dissolved minerals downwards out of a soil layer by rainwater.", title="Leaching"),
            T("In hot, wet climates, such as the forest of southern Cameroon, heavy rain leaches away many nutrients and silica. Iron and aluminium oxides remain, which give the soil its red colour. A very leached, iron-rich tropical soil is often called a **laterite** soil. When it dries and hardens it can form a hard crust. The forest looks rich, but most nutrients are held in living plants and in the thin layer of leaf litter, not in the soil. Textbooks use different names for tropical soils, so check yours. Soils on young volcanic rocks, such as those on the slopes of Mount Cameroon, are generally more fertile because fresh volcanic minerals keep supplying nutrients."),
            EX("Worked example 1: reading a profile", intro="In a pit, the top 5 cm is dark leaf litter. Below it is a dark brown layer with many roots down to 30 cm. From 30 cm to 80 cm the soil is red-brown and sticky. From 80 cm to 140 cm there are angular pieces of rock in a brown matrix. Below 140 cm the rock is solid. Name each layer.", steps=[
                S("Top 5 cm", text="Leaf litter and humus: O horizon."),
                S("5 to 30 cm", text="Dark with many roots: A horizon (topsoil)."),
                S("30 to 80 cm", text="Red-brown clayey layer where washed-in materials collect: B horizon (subsoil)."),
                S("80 to 140 cm", text="Broken parent rock in soil: C horizon."),
                S("Below 140 cm", text="Solid unweathered rock: R, the bedrock."),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: pore space", intro="A soil core of volume 500 cm3 is oven-dried and its solid grains occupy 275 cm3. What percentage of the core is pore space (spaces holding air and water)?", steps=[
                S("Pore volume", math=r"500 - 275 = 225 \text{ cm}^3"),
                S("Percentage", math=r"\frac{225}{500} \times 100 = 45\%"),
                S("Meaning", text="Nearly half of the soil is empty space. These pores store water and air that roots need."),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Calling soil 'broken rock'", "Soil also contains humus, water and air. Without humus and living organisms, loose rock debris is just weathered rock, not true soil."),
            MISTAKE("Putting the B horizon at the top", "The order from the top is O, A, B, C, R. The A horizon is topsoil; the B horizon is subsoil below it."),
            QUIZ("Which horizon is the topsoil that contains most humus and roots?", ["B horizon", "C horizon", "A horizon", "Bedrock"], 2, "The A horizon is the topsoil, dark with humus and full of roots.", hint="Which letter comes first among A, B and C?", feedback=["The B horizon is subsoil where washed-in materials collect.", "The C horizon is partly weathered rock with little humus.", "", "Bedrock is solid rock."]),
            QUIZ("A tropical soil is red and poor in nutrients. Which process explains the loss of nutrients?", ["Leaching by heavy rain", "Freezing and thawing", "Deposition by glaciers", "Wind erosion in a desert"], 0, "Heavy rain washes dissolved nutrients and silica downwards, leaving iron oxides.", hint="Think of strong rain on warm soil.", feedback=["", "There is no freezing in the lowland tropics.", "Glaciers are not part of this setting.", "Desert wind is not involved in a rainforest soil."]),
            TIP("Soil profile questions can be a labelled or unlabelled diagram in Paper 1 or a structured question in Paper 2. Learn the order O, A, B, C, R and one feature of each layer.", title="Exam link"),
            PQ("Define soil and name its four components.", "Soil is the loose layer at the surface in which plants grow. Its components are weathered rock particles (minerals), humus (organic matter), water and air."),
            PQ("List the five factors that control the formation of soil.", "Parent material, climate, relief (slope), organisms and time."),
            PQ("Why are many tropical forest soils red and low in nutrients even though the forest is rich?", "Heavy rain leaches dissolved nutrients and silica downwards, leaving iron and aluminium oxides that make the soil red. Most nutrients are held in the living plants and the thin layer of leaf litter."),
            SUM("Soil is weathered rock plus humus, water and air.",
                "Parent material, climate, relief, organisms and time control soil formation.",
                "A profile has horizons O, A, B, C and bedrock R.",
                "Leaching in hot, wet climates leaves red, iron-rich, low-nutrient tropical soils."),
        ],
    ),
    lesson(
        module=M4, topic="Mass wasting",
        title="Mass wasting: creep, slump, mudflow and rockfall",
        slug="gly2-mass-wasting",
        minutes=20,
        summary="Mass wasting is the downslope movement of soil and rock under gravity. Creep is slow; slump, mudflow and rockfall are faster. Steep slopes, heavy rain, weak rock and removed vegetation make slopes fail.",
        objectives=[
            "Define mass wasting and state that gravity is the driving force",
            "Describe creep, slump, mudflow and rockfall and name the evidence for each",
            "Explain how water, slope angle and vegetation affect slope stability",
            "Suggest ways of reducing landslide danger on a hillside",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Mass wasting", "Creep", "Slump", "Mudflow", "Rockfall", "Angle of repose", "Slope stability"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("Every rainy season, news from the hill towns of Cameroon includes roads blocked by earth that has slid across them. Houses built on steep slopes can be damaged. All of this is **mass wasting**: soil and rock moving downhill because of gravity, with no river, glacier or wind carrying it."),
            D("**Mass wasting** (or mass movement) is the downslope movement of soil, regolith and rock under the force of **gravity**. Water often helps, but it is not the carrier: the material moves as a mass.", title="Mass wasting"),
            T("**Regolith** is the loose layer of weathered rock and soil above solid bedrock. Mass wasting moves regolith and sometimes bedrock blocks."),
            H("The four main types"),
            KEY("Types of mass wasting", "Speed and water content separate the types.", cards=[
                ["Creep", "Very slow movement of soil, a few millimetres or centimetres a year. Signs: fence posts and trees that lean downhill, curved tree trunks, cracked walls and tilted poles."],
                ["Slump", "A block of soil or weak rock slides down a **curved surface**, rotating backwards. Signs: a curved scar (scarp) at the top and a bulging heap at the bottom."],
                ["Mudflow", "A fast flow of soil saturated with water, moving like thick wet cement down a valley. Common after very heavy rain."],
                ["Rockfall", "Rock fragments fall freely from a steep cliff and gather as a heap of loose rock (talus or scree) at the base."]]),
            H("What makes a slope fail?"),
            D("The **angle of repose** is the steepest angle at which loose material remains stable. Above this angle the material slides. Dry sand has an angle of repose of about 30 to 35 degrees; the exact figure depends on grain size and shape.", title="Angle of repose"),
            KEY("Causes of slope failure", "A slope fails when the pull of gravity is larger than the strength holding it.", cards=[
                ["Water", "Rain fills pores, adds weight and reduces friction between grains. This is the most common trigger."],
                ["Steepness", "A steeper slope increases the downslope pull. Building a road that cuts into a hill makes it steeper."],
                ["Weak material", "Clay, weathered rock and loose sediment fail easily. Layers of rock sloping with the surface can slide."],
                ["Loss of vegetation", "Roots bind soil and take up water. Cutting trees weakens the slope."],
                ["Shaking", "Earthquakes and heavy traffic or blasting can start a slide."]]),
            EX("Worked example 1: identify the type", intro="Name the type of mass wasting. (a) A curved scar appears on a hillside behind a house and the ground below it has dropped and rotated. (b) Boulders lie in a heap at the foot of a quarry face. (c) Over many years a hillside power pole leans downhill.", steps=[
                S("(a) Curved scar, block rotated", text="Slump."),
                S("(b) Heap of loose rock under a cliff", text="Rockfall (the heap is talus)."),
                S("(c) Slow tilting with no sudden break", text="Creep."),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: model answer", intro="Explain why a hillside slope failed after three days of heavy rain.", steps=[
                S("Water enters", text="Rain infiltrates the soil and fills the pores."),
                S("Weight increases and friction drops", text="The soil becomes heavier and the water lubricates the contact between grains and layers."),
                S("Gravity wins", text="The downslope pull exceeds the strength of the soil, so the soil moves as a slump or mudflow."),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Saying a river carries a landslide", "Mass wasting is moved by gravity as a mass. When the moving material is carried by flowing water in channels, that is river transport, not mass wasting."),
            MISTAKE("Thinking creep is fast", "Creep is the slowest type, often too slow to see. The signs are leaning posts, trees and tilted poles."),
            QUIZ("Which mass movement is a fast flow of very wet soil down a valley?", ["Creep", "Slump", "Mudflow", "Rockfall"], 2, "A mudflow is soil saturated with water that flows like wet cement.", hint="Think of mud.", feedback=["Creep is very slow.", "Slump is a rotation of a block on a curved surface.", "", "Rockfall is falling rock, not flowing mud."]),
            QUIZ("Which action would most reduce the risk of a landslide on a steep farm slope?", ["Cutting all trees to plant crops", "Planting deep-rooted trees and draining water away", "Digging the toe of the slope away", "Building a house at the top edge"], 1, "Roots bind soil and drains remove water.", hint="Which action strengthens the slope?", feedback=["Removing trees weakens the slope.", "", "Removing the toe makes the slope steeper and unsupported.", "Adding weight at the top increases the pull."]),
            TIP("Mass wasting is tested in Paper 1 questions asking for the type from a description or a cause. Learn one sign for each type and remember that gravity is the driving force.", title="Exam link"),
            PQ("Define mass wasting.", "Mass wasting is the downslope movement of soil and rock under the force of gravity."),
            PQ("Describe two differences between creep and slump.", "Creep is very slow and continuous, and shows as leaning trees and poles. Slump is a sudden movement of a block along a curved surface and leaves a curved scar at the top."),
            PQ("Give three factors that increase the chance of a landslide.", "Heavy rainfall that saturates the soil, steep slopes or slopes steepened by cutting, removal of vegetation, weak or clay-rich materials and earthquakes are valid factors."),
            SUM("Mass wasting is the downslope movement of material under gravity.",
                "Creep is slow, slump rotates on a curved surface, mudflow is a wet flow, rockfall is free fall.",
                "Water, steepness, weak material, lost vegetation and shaking trigger failures.",
                "Vegetation, drainage and gentle slopes reduce the risk."),
        ],
    ),
    lesson(
        module=M4, topic="Rivers and their landforms",
        title="Rivers: erosion, transport, deposition and landforms",
        slug="gly2-rivers-erosion-transport-deposition",
        minutes=24,
        summary="Rivers erode (hydraulic action, abrasion, attrition, solution), carry a load (solution, suspension, saltation, traction) and deposit it. They make V-shaped valleys and waterfalls upstream, and meanders, oxbow lakes, floodplains and deltas downstream.",
        objectives=[
            "Describe four ways a river erodes and four ways it transports its load",
            "Explain when and why a river deposits",
            "Describe V-shaped valleys, waterfalls, meanders, oxbow lakes, floodplains, levees and deltas and how they form",
            "Calculate river discharge from width, depth and velocity",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Hydraulic action", "Abrasion", "Attrition", "Load", "Saltation", "Meander", "Oxbow lake", "Floodplain", "Delta", "Discharge"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("A river such as the Sanaga or the Wouri begins as small streams on high ground and ends at the sea. On the way it cuts valleys, rolls stones, carries mud and builds flat land beside it. Most of the landforms you see in valleys were made by running water."),
            H("Erosion"),
            KEY("How a river erodes", "Rivers wear away their beds and banks in four ways.", cards=[
                ["Hydraulic action", "The force of moving water pushes into cracks and breaks rock and loosens bank material."],
                ["Abrasion (corrasion)", "The river's load of sand and stones scrapes and grinds the bed and banks, like sandpaper."],
                ["Attrition", "Stones knock against each other and become smaller and rounder."],
                ["Solution (corrosion)", "Slightly acidic water dissolves soluble rock such as limestone."]]),
            H("Transport"),
            D("A river's **load** is all the material it carries. It moves in four ways: in **solution** (dissolved ions), in **suspension** (fine silt and clay floating in the water), by **saltation** (sand grains bouncing along the bed) and by **traction** (large pebbles and boulders rolling or sliding along the bed).", title="River load"),
            T("When the river floods, its speed and volume rise, and it can carry larger pieces. A brown, muddy river in the rainy season is carrying much more suspended load than in the dry season."),
            H("Deposition"),
            D("**Deposition** is the dropping of the load when the river loses energy: when its speed falls, as on the inside of a bend, on a flat floodplain, when it enters a lake or the sea, or when it floods and spreads out. The largest particles are dropped first.", title="Deposition"),
            H("Landforms of the upper course"),
            T("Near its source a river is steep and fast. It cuts **downwards** and forms a **V-shaped valley**, with **interlocking spurs** where the river bends around ridges. A **waterfall** forms where the river flows over a layer of hard rock that lies above softer rock. The soft rock below is eroded faster, leaving a step. The water plunges over the step into a **plunge pool**, and the waterfall slowly moves upstream."),
            H("Landforms of the middle and lower course"),
            IMG("f5gly2-river.svg", alt="Plan view of a meandering river on its floodplain, with the river cliff on the outside of one bend, the slip-off slope on the inside, and an oxbow lake", caption="Figure 1. A meandering river and an oxbow lake. Flow is left to right."),
            KEY("Meanders and their relatives", "In the lower course the river swings from side to side.", cards=[
                ["Meander", "A bend in the river. On the **outer bank** the current is fast, so erosion makes a steep **river cliff**. On the **inner bank** the water is slow, so sand and gravel are deposited and form a gentle **slip-off slope**."],
                ["Oxbow lake", "As a meander grows, its neck gets narrower. In a flood, the river cuts through the neck and takes the short route. Deposits seal the ends of the old bend, leaving a curved lake."],
                ["Floodplain", "The wide, flat land beside a river, built from silt left by floods. It is fertile and often farmed."],
                ["Levee", "A low natural ridge beside the channel. Heavy material drops first when floodwater leaves the channel."],
                ["Delta", "A low area of land built where a river enters a lake or sea and drops its load. Its shape depends on the river and the waves and tides."]]),
            EX("Worked example 1: discharge", intro="A stream is 8 m wide and has an average depth of 1.5 m. The average velocity of the water is 0.5 m per second. Find the discharge (volume of water passing per second).", steps=[
                S("Formula", math=r"\text{discharge} = \text{width} \times \text{depth} \times \text{velocity}"),
                S("Substitute", math=r"8 \times 1.5 \times 0.5"),
                S("Answer", math=r"6 \text{ m}^3 \text{ per second}"),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: how an oxbow lake forms", intro="Describe, in order, how an oxbow lake forms.", steps=[
                S("Erosion on the outer bank, deposition on the inner bank", text="The meander becomes larger and the neck between two bends becomes narrow."),
                S("Flood", text="At high flow the river breaks through the neck and takes the shorter path."),
                S("Sealing", text="Deposits block the ends of the old bend, which is left as a curved lake."),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Mixing up the two banks of a meander", "River cliff = **outer** bank = **erosion**. Slip-off slope = **inner** bank = **deposition**. Remember: fast water on the outside digs, slow water on the inside drops sand."),
            MISTAKE("Saying attrition makes the river bed deeper", "Attrition wears the **load** itself. Abrasion and hydraulic action wear the bed and banks."),
            QUIZ("Large boulders rolled along the river bed are carried by", ["Solution", "Suspension", "Saltation", "Traction"], 3, "Traction is the rolling or sliding of the heaviest load along the bed.", hint="Which method needs the most energy?", feedback=["Solution carries dissolved ions.", "Suspension carries fine particles in the water.", "Saltation is bouncing of sand grains.", ""]),
            QUIZ("Where on a meander is sand deposited?", ["The outer bank, where the water is fastest", "The inner bank, where the water is slowest", "Only at the source of the river", "Only on the sea bed"], 1, "Slow water on the inside of the bend loses energy and drops its load, making the slip-off slope.", hint="Where is the current weakest?", feedback=["The outer bank is eroded, not built up.", "", "Deposition can occur anywhere along the river where energy is low, not only at the source.", "Deposition occurs in many places before the sea is reached."]),
            TIP("River landforms and their formation are tested in Paper 1 questions and diagram questions in Paper 2. Practise labelling a meander, a waterfall and a delta from an unlabelled sketch.", title="Exam link"),
            PQ("Name the four ways in which a river transports its load.", "Solution, suspension, saltation and traction."),
            PQ("Explain how a waterfall forms.", "Hard rock lies above softer rock across the river's path. The soft rock is eroded faster and a step forms. Water falls over the step into a plunge pool, which undercuts the hard rock. The overhang collapses and the waterfall retreats upstream."),
            PQ("A river is 12 m wide and 2 m deep and flows at 0.75 m per second. Calculate its discharge.", "12 x 2 x 0.75 = 18 cubic metres per second."),
            SUM("A river erodes by hydraulic action, abrasion, attrition and solution.",
                "It carries its load in solution, suspension, saltation and traction, and deposits it when it loses energy.",
                "Upper course: V-shaped valley, interlocking spurs and waterfalls.",
                "Lower course: meanders, oxbow lakes, floodplains, levees and deltas. Discharge = width x depth x velocity."),
        ],
    ),
    lesson(
        module=M4, topic="Wind, ice and the sea",
        title="Wind, ice and the sea as agents of landscape change",
        slug="gly2-wind-ice-sea-landforms",
        minutes=22,
        summary="Wind makes deflation hollows, dunes and loess; glaciers carve cirques, arêtes and U-shaped valleys and drop moraines; waves cut cliffs and platforms and build beaches and spits. Cameroon has no glaciers now, but it has a long Atlantic coast.",
        objectives=[
            "Describe how wind erodes, transports and deposits (deflation, abrasion, dunes, loess)",
            "Describe the main erosional and depositional landforms of glaciers",
            "Describe how waves erode and deposit, and name coastal landforms",
            "Match each agent to a landform in a diagram or description",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Deflation", "Abrasion", "Dune", "Loess", "Glacier", "Cirque", "U-shaped valley", "Moraine", "Wave-cut platform", "Spit"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("Wind, ice and the sea shape landscapes that look very different from each other. Great sand dunes form in deserts such as the Sahara, to the north of Cameroon. Glaciers once covered much of Europe and shaped its valleys. The sea works every day along the Atlantic coast near Limbe and Kribi, wearing rock and moving sand. This lesson compares the three agents."),
            H("Wind (aeolian) processes"),
            KEY("What wind does", "Wind is strongest in dry regions with little plant cover and loose sand.", cards=[
                ["Deflation", "Wind blows away loose fine particles and lowers the ground surface. It can leave a hollow (deflation hollow) or a pavement of stones that are too heavy to move."],
                ["Abrasion", "Wind-blown sand grains sandblast rock surfaces and polish and wear them. It works mainly near the ground, because sand rarely rises high."],
                ["Transport", "Sand bounces along the ground in saltation; fine dust is carried high in suspension; some grains creep along the surface."],
                ["Deposition", "When the wind speed drops, the load is dropped: sand forms **dunes**, and very fine dust forms deposits of **loess**."]]),
            D("A **dune** is a mound or ridge of wind-blown sand. It has a gentle windward slope up which sand is blown, and a steeper slip face on the sheltered side where sand slides down. Dunes slowly move downwind.", title="Dune"),
            H("Ice (glacial) processes"),
            D("A **glacier** is a large mass of ice that moves slowly downhill under its own weight on land. Glaciers erode by **plucking** (freezing to rock and pulling pieces away) and **abrasion** (rock fragments frozen into the ice scratch the rock beneath it).", title="Glacier"),
            KEY("Glacial landforms", "Ice makes distinct shapes in mountains and lowlands.", cards=[
                ["Cirque (corrie)", "An armchair-shaped hollow high on a mountain where a glacier began."],
                ["Arête", "A sharp ridge left between two cirques or two glaciated valleys."],
                ["U-shaped valley", "A steep-sided, flat-floored valley made by a glacier. A river valley is V-shaped; a glacier widens and deepens it into a U."],
                ["Moraine", "Rock debris dropped by ice, such as ridges at the sides and end of a glacier."],
                ["Erratic", "A boulder carried by ice far from its source and dropped on different rock."]]),
            T("There are no glaciers in Cameroon today. Some African mountains, such as Mount Kenya and Kilimanjaro, have small ice caps, but they are far from Cameroon. For Cameroon students, the glacial landforms are a study of other regions and past ice ages, so use the diagrams in your textbook."),
            H("The sea (marine) processes"),
            D("Waves erode by **hydraulic action** (air and water forced into cracks), **abrasion** (waves throwing sand and shingle at the cliff) and **attrition**. They also deposit where wave energy is low.", title="Wave action"),
            KEY("Coastal landforms", "Erosion forms rocky shores; deposition forms sandy ones.", cards=[
                ["Cliff and wave-cut platform", "Waves cut a notch at the base of a cliff. The cliff above collapses and retreats, leaving a flat rock surface called a wave-cut platform."],
                ["Arch and stack", "Waves widen a crack in a headland into a cave, then an arch. When the arch collapses, a rock column is left, called a stack."],
                ["Beach", "A strip of sand or shingle deposited by waves between the low-tide and high-tide marks."],
                ["Spit", "A long ridge of sand or shingle attached to the land at one end, built where longshore drift carries sediment past a bend in the coast."]]),
            EX("Worked example 1: match agent and landform", intro="State the agent that formed each landform: (a) cirque, (b) stack, (c) dune, (d) U-shaped valley, (e) loess.", steps=[
                S("(a) Cirque", text="Glacier (ice)."),
                S("(b) Stack", text="The sea (waves)."),
                S("(c) Dune", text="Wind."),
                S("(d) U-shaped valley", text="Glacier (ice)."),
                S("(e) Loess", text="Wind (fine dust)."),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: model answer", intro="Describe how a stack is formed.", steps=[
                S("Crack", text="Waves attack a crack in a headland by hydraulic action and abrasion."),
                S("Cave and arch", text="The crack widens into a cave. The cave is cut through the headland to form an arch."),
                S("Collapse", text="The arch roof is weakened and falls, leaving a pillar of rock called a stack."),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Giving glacial landforms for Cameroon", "Cameroon has no glaciers now. If a question asks about a landform in Cameroon, think about rivers, volcanoes, wind-blown sand in the far north or coastal landforms."),
            MISTAKE("Mixing up deflation and abrasion", "Deflation is **removal** of loose particles by wind. Abrasion is **wearing** of rock by the particles that the wind carries."),
            QUIZ("Which feature is formed by the deposition of fine wind-blown dust?", ["Dune", "Loess", "Spit", "Moraine"], 1, "Loess is a deposit of fine wind-blown silt.", hint="Think of fine dust, not sand.", feedback=["A dune is made of sand, not fine dust.", "", "A spit is made by the sea.", "A moraine is made by ice."]),
            QUIZ("A river valley is V-shaped. After a glacier occupies it, the valley becomes", ["Narrower and V-shaped", "A cirque", "A U-shaped valley", "An oxbow lake"], 2, "The ice widens and deepens the valley into a steep-sided U-shaped trough.", hint="The shape of the letter that follows T in the alphabet.", feedback=["A glacier widens the valley.", "A cirque is a hollow at the head of a glacier.", "", "An oxbow lake is a river feature."]),
            TIP("Landforms of wind, ice and the sea are tested by matching agent to landform in Paper 1, and as a diagram labelling question in Paper 2. Learn one process and one landform for each agent.", title="Exam link"),
            PQ("Describe how wind sand forms a dune.", "Wind carries sand by saltation. When it meets an obstacle or loses speed, it drops the sand. A heap builds with a gentle windward slope and a steep slip face on the sheltered side."),
            PQ("Name two erosional processes of a glacier and one landform that they form.", "Plucking and abrasion. They form cirques, arêtes and U-shaped valleys."),
            PQ("Explain how a wave-cut platform forms.", "Waves cut a notch at the foot of a cliff by hydraulic action and abrasion. The cliff above collapses and retreats. The flat rock surface left at the base is the wave-cut platform."),
            SUM("Wind erodes by deflation and abrasion and deposits dunes and loess.",
                "Glaciers erode by plucking and abrasion and make cirques, arêtes and U-shaped valleys, and deposit moraines.",
                "Waves erode cliffs and platforms (and arches and stacks) and deposit beaches and spits.",
                "Cameroon has no glaciers today, so glacial landforms come from other regions."),
        ],
    ),
    lesson(
        module=M5, topic="Stress, strain, strike and dip",
        title="Stress, strain, strike and dip",
        slug="gly2-stress-strain-strike-dip",
        minutes=24,
        summary="Stress is force on rock; strain is the change it causes. Rocks deform elastically, plastically or by breaking. Strike is the direction of a level line on a bed and dip is its angle of tilt, at right angles to strike.",
        objectives=[
            "Define stress and strain and name compressional, tensional and shear stress",
            "Distinguish elastic, ductile (plastic) and brittle deformation",
            "Define strike and dip and read a strike and dip symbol on a map",
            "Calculate the true thickness of a dipping bed from its outcrop width on flat ground",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Stress", "Strain", "Compression", "Tension", "Shear", "Strike", "Dip", "Dip direction", "True thickness"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("Press a ruler between your hands and it bends. Press harder and it snaps. Rocks behave in the same way, but over millions of years. The forces come from moving tectonic plates. To study the result, geologists measure how beds are tilted. That is what **strike and dip** are for, and map questions on the examination depend on them."),
            D("**Stress** is a force applied to a rock, measured as force per unit area. **Strain** is the change in shape, volume or position of the rock that the stress produces.", title="Stress and strain", formula=r"\text{stress} = \frac{\text{force}}{\text{area}}"),
            KEY("Three kinds of stress", "The direction of the force gives the type.", cards=[
                ["Compression", "Rock is squeezed. Rocks shorten and thicken. Typical at colliding plates. Often makes folds and reverse faults."],
                ["Tension", "Rock is pulled apart. Rocks lengthen and thin. Typical at diverging plates. Often makes normal faults."],
                ["Shear", "Rock is pushed in opposite directions along a plane, like sliding the top of a book one way and the bottom the other. Makes strike-slip faults."]]),
            KEY("How rocks respond", "The response depends on the rock, temperature, pressure and the speed of loading.", cards=[
                ["Elastic", "The rock returns to its original shape when the stress is removed. Seismic waves pass through rock this way."],
                ["Ductile (plastic)", "The rock bends and flows, keeping the new shape. Common in hot, deep rock and under slow stress. Makes folds."],
                ["Brittle", "The rock breaks. Common in cold, shallow, strong rock under fast stress. Makes faults and joints."]]),
            H("Strike and dip"),
            T("A sedimentary bed that was laid down flat is often tilted later. To describe the tilt, geologists measure two things with a compass-clinometer."),
            D("**Strike** is the compass direction of a horizontal line drawn on the surface of an inclined bed. A line of equal height on the bed. **Dip** is the angle between the bed surface and the horizontal, measured at **right angles** to the strike. The **dip direction** is the direction in which the bed slopes downward.", title="Strike and dip"),
            IMG("f5gly2-strike-dip.svg", alt="A plan view with a vertical strike line and an arrow showing dip direction to the east with the angle 30 beside it, and a cross-section showing the dip angle between the horizontal and the tilted bed surface", caption="Figure 1. Strike line, dip direction and dip angle."),
            KEY("Writing and reading strike and dip", "Use the three facts together.", cards=[
                ["On a map", "A long line shows the strike. A short tick on one side points in the dip direction. A number beside it gives the dip angle in degrees."],
                ["Right angles", "The dip direction is always at 90 degrees to the strike. Strike north-south means dip east or west. Strike east-west means dip north or south."],
                ["Written form", "For example 'strike north-south, dip 30 degrees east', or, with bearings, strike 000 degrees, dip 30 degrees towards 090 degrees."]]),
            T("A **flat** bed has a dip of 0 degrees and has no strike. A **vertical** bed has a dip of 90 degrees. A steep dip means the beds are strongly tilted."),
            H("True thickness from a map"),
            T("On flat ground, a bed that dips at an angle shows a band on the map. The band's width across the strike is **not** the real thickness of the bed. The real, or **true thickness**, is measured at right angles to the bed surfaces. For flat ground:"),
            F(r"\text{true thickness} = \text{outcrop width} \times \sin(\text{dip})", title="True thickness on flat ground"),
            EX("Worked example 1: strike and dip direction", intro="A bed strikes north-south and dips to the east at 35 degrees. Another bed strikes east-west and dips to the south at 20 degrees. State the dip direction of each and the strike of each.", steps=[
                S("First bed", text="Strike north-south, so dip direction is east or west. It is given as east: dip 35 degrees east."),
                S("Second bed", text="Strike east-west, so dip direction is north or south. It is given as south: dip 20 degrees south."),
                S("Check right angles", text="North-south and east are at 90 degrees. East-west and south are at 90 degrees."),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: true thickness", intro="A sandstone bed outcrops as a band 200 m wide on flat ground and dips at 30 degrees. What is its true thickness?", steps=[
                S("Formula", math=r"\text{thickness} = w \sin(\text{dip})"),
                S("Substitute", math=r"200 \times \sin 30^\circ = 200 \times 0.5"),
                S("Answer", math=r"100 \text{ m}"),
            ], check=r"200 \sin 30^\circ = 100"),
            MISTAKE("Saying dip is the direction of strike", "Strike is the direction of a level line. Dip is the **angle** of slope, measured at right angles to strike, with a direction."),
            MISTAKE("Calling the outcrop width the thickness", "The outcrop width is only the true thickness when the bed is vertical (dip 90 degrees). For a lesser dip, multiply by sin of the dip angle."),
            QUIZ("A bed has strike east-west. Which dip direction is possible?", ["East", "West", "North", "North-east"], 2, "The dip direction is at right angles to strike, so with strike east-west it is north or south.", hint="Draw the strike line, then draw a line at right angles.", feedback=["East is along the strike, not at right angles.", "West is along the strike.", "", "North-east makes 45 degrees, not 90."]),
            QUIZ("Which type of stress mainly produces folds and reverse faults?", ["Tension", "Compression", "Shear only", "No stress"], 1, "Compression shortens the crust, folding and thrusting it.", hint="Think of two plates colliding.", feedback=["Tension pulls rocks apart and forms normal faults.", "", "Shear produces strike-slip faults.", "Deformation needs stress."]),
            TIP("Strike and dip are tested in Paper 1 and in the map question of Paper 2. The Board's examiners reported that candidates found map work hard, especially drawing a profile along a given line and relating the beds to contour lines. Practise reading strike and dip symbols.", title="Exam link"),
            PQ("Define stress and strain.", "Stress is a force acting on a rock per unit area. Strain is the change in shape, size or position that the rock undergoes in response."),
            PQ("Define strike and dip.", "Strike is the compass direction of a horizontal line on an inclined bed surface. Dip is the angle between the bed and the horizontal, measured at right angles to the strike, in the direction in which the bed goes down."),
            PQ("A bed outcrops as a band 150 m wide on flat ground and dips at 40 degrees. Calculate its true thickness to the nearest metre.", "True thickness = 150 x sin 40 degrees = 150 x 0.643 = 96 m (to the nearest metre)."),
            SUM("Stress is force per unit area; strain is the change it causes.",
                "Compression, tension and shear make folds, normal faults and strike-slip faults. Rocks respond elastically, by flowing (ductile) or by breaking (brittle).",
                "Strike is the direction of a level line on a bed; dip is the angle of tilt at right angles to strike.",
                "On flat ground, true thickness = outcrop width x sin(dip)."),
        ],
    ),
    lesson(
        module=M5, topic="Folds",
        title="Folds: anticlines, synclines and their parts",
        slug="gly2-folds-anticline-syncline",
        minutes=22,
        summary="Folds are bends in rock layers made by compression. An anticline arches up with its limbs dipping away and the oldest rock in the core; a syncline sags with limbs dipping inward and the youngest rock in the core. Folds can be symmetrical, asymmetrical, overturned or recumbent.",
        objectives=[
            "Define a fold and name its parts: limb, hinge, axial plane, axis",
            "Distinguish an anticline from a syncline by shape, dip and age of the beds",
            "Describe symmetrical, asymmetrical, overturned and recumbent folds",
            "Calculate the shortening produced by folding",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Fold", "Anticline", "Syncline", "Limb", "Hinge", "Axial plane", "Overturned fold", "Recumbent fold", "Plunge"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("Push the two ends of a table cloth towards each other and it rises into ridges and sinks into troughs. Rock layers do the same when the crust is squeezed. Where the rock is hot and deep, it bends instead of breaking. The result is a **fold**."),
            D("A **fold** is a bend in rock layers (or other planar features) produced by permanent, plastic deformation, usually under compression.", title="Fold"),
            IMG("f5gly2-folds.svg", alt="Cross-section of folded layers showing an anticline on the left and a syncline on the right, the dashed axial planes and a limb", caption="Figure 1. An anticline and a syncline. Dashed lines are the axial planes."),
            KEY("The two basic folds", "Both are named from the shape and the dip of the limbs.", cards=[
                ["Anticline", "An upfold, an arch. Both limbs dip **away** from the axis. After erosion, the **oldest** rocks are in the **core** (centre) with younger rocks on the outside."],
                ["Syncline", "A downfold, a trough. Both limbs dip **towards** the axis. After erosion, the **youngest** rocks are in the **core**, with older rocks on the outside."]]),
            T("This rule about ages assumes the layers are in their original order, with the oldest at the bottom. It can be reversed in very strongly folded rock, so always ask which way is up."),
            H("The parts of a fold"),
            KEY("Naming a fold", "The same words are used for every fold.", cards=[
                ["Limb", "The side of a fold, between a hinge and the next hinge."],
                ["Hinge (crest or trough)", "The line of greatest curvature on a folded surface; the highest line on an anticline (crest) or the lowest on a syncline (trough)."],
                ["Axial plane", "An imaginary plane that divides the fold into two limbs as symmetrically as possible. It passes through all the hinges."],
                ["Fold axis", "The line where the axial plane meets a folded surface. If the axis is tilted (not horizontal), the fold **plunges**."]]),
            H("Types of fold by shape"),
            KEY("Describing the symmetry", "The tilt of the axial plane gives the type.", cards=[
                ["Symmetrical", "The axial plane is vertical and the limbs dip at equal angles."],
                ["Asymmetrical", "The axial plane is tilted and the limbs dip at different angles."],
                ["Overturned", "The axial plane is tilted so far that one limb has been turned upside down. Both limbs dip in the same direction."],
                ["Recumbent", "The axial plane is almost horizontal, so the fold lies on its side."]]),
            T("A **monocline** is a single step-like bend in otherwise flat beds. A **dome** is a fold in which beds dip away in all directions from a central high point, and a **basin** is a fold in which they dip towards the centre. Check your textbook for the use of these terms."),
            EX("Worked example 1: anticline or syncline?", intro="A traverse from west to east crosses these rocks at the surface: young shale, then older sandstone, then oldest limestone, then older sandstone, then young shale. Beds are in original order. What structure is it?", steps=[
                S("Look at the centre", text="The oldest rock (limestone) is in the middle."),
                S("Apply the rule", text="Oldest in the core means an anticline."),
                S("Check the limbs", text="Beds on the west would dip west and on the east would dip east, away from the axis, which matches an anticline."),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: shortening", intro="A limestone bed was originally 10 km long. After folding, the same bed spans only 8 km measured along the ground. What is the percentage shortening?", steps=[
                S("Difference in length", math=r"10 - 8 = 2 \text{ km}"),
                S("Percentage", math=r"\frac{2}{10} \times 100 = 20\%"),
                S("Meaning", text="Compression has shortened the crust by 20 per cent in this direction."),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Saying the anticline has the youngest rock in the core", "The oldest rocks are in the core of an anticline, the youngest in the core of a syncline. (Reverse it for tops-down, overturned layers.)"),
            MISTAKE("Mixing up limb and axial plane", "A limb is a **side** of the fold. The axial plane is the surface that divides it into two limbs."),
            QUIZ("A fold has both limbs dipping towards the axial plane. It is a", ["Anticline", "Syncline", "Dome", "Horst"], 1, "Limbs dipping towards the axis make a downfold, a syncline.", hint="Do the limbs dip towards or away?", feedback=["Anticline limbs dip away from the axis.", "", "A dome dips away from a point in all directions.", "A horst is a fault block, not a fold."]),
            QUIZ("In a recumbent fold the axial plane is", ["Vertical", "Almost horizontal", "Absent", "Curved like a limb"], 1, "A recumbent fold lies on its side, so its axial plane is nearly horizontal.", hint="Recumbent means lying down.", feedback=["A vertical axial plane belongs to a symmetrical fold.", "", "Every fold has an axial plane.", "An axial plane is a plane, not part of the limb."]),
            TIP("Fold diagrams and the age of beds in a fold are tested in Paper 1 and in the diagram and map questions of Paper 2. Learn the rule 'anticline: oldest in the core; syncline: youngest in the core'.", title="Exam link"),
            PQ("Define an anticline and a syncline.", "An anticline is an upfold whose limbs dip away from the axis, with the oldest rock in the core. A syncline is a downfold whose limbs dip towards the axis, with the youngest rock in the core."),
            PQ("Name the type of stress that forms folds and state whether the rocks respond in a brittle or ductile way.", "Compression forms folds. The rocks respond in a ductile (plastic) way, bending instead of breaking."),
            PQ("A rock layer 25 km long is folded so that it spans 20 km. Calculate the percentage shortening.", "Shortening = (25 - 20) / 25 x 100 = 20 per cent."),
            SUM("A fold is a bend in rock layers, usually made by compression.",
                "Anticline: limbs dip away, oldest rock in the core. Syncline: limbs dip inward, youngest rock in the core.",
                "Parts: limb, hinge, axial plane and axis (plunge when tilted).",
                "Types: symmetrical, asymmetrical, overturned and recumbent."),
        ],
    ),
    lesson(
        module=M5, topic="Faults, joints and unconformities",
        title="Faults, joints and unconformities",
        slug="gly2-faults-joints-unconformities",
        minutes=26,
        summary="A fault is a fracture with movement; a joint has none. Normal, reverse and strike-slip faults come from tension, compression and shear. A horst is a raised block and a graben a dropped block. Unconformities are gaps in the record: angular, parallel and heterolithic.",
        objectives=[
            "Define fault, joint, hanging wall and footwall and tell fault from joint",
            "Identify normal, reverse and strike-slip faults and horst and graben",
            "Define angular, parallel and heterolithic unconformities as the Board's examiners do",
            "Explain how an unconformity forms in four steps",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Fault", "Joint", "Hanging wall", "Footwall", "Normal fault", "Reverse fault", "Strike-slip fault", "Horst", "Graben", "Unconformity"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("A road cut in a hillside shows a line along which layers do not match: a layer on one side sits higher than the same layer on the other side. Rock has broken and slid. This lesson covers breaks in the rock (faults and joints) and gaps in the story the rocks tell (unconformities)."),
            D("A **fault** is a fracture in rock along which the two sides have moved relative to each other. A **joint** is a fracture along which there has been **no** noticeable movement. The **fault plane** is the surface of the fracture.", title="Fault and joint"),
            D("On a **dipping** fault plane, the **hanging wall** is the block above the plane and the **footwall** is the block below it. (Miners hung their lamps on the block above and walked on the one below.)", title="Hanging wall and footwall"),
            IMG("f5gly2-faults.svg", alt="Two cross-sections side by side: a normal fault where the hanging wall has moved down and a reverse fault where the hanging wall has moved up", caption="Figure 1. A normal fault and a reverse fault. Check each time which block is the hanging wall."),
            KEY("Types of fault", "The movement of the hanging wall decides the name.", cards=[
                ["Normal fault", "The hanging wall moves **down** relative to the footwall. Caused by **tension** (the crust is stretched). The crust is lengthened."],
                ["Reverse fault", "The hanging wall moves **up** relative to the footwall. Caused by **compression**. The crust is shortened. A reverse fault with a gentle dip is called a **thrust**."],
                ["Strike-slip fault", "Blocks slide **sideways** past each other along a nearly vertical fault plane. Caused by **shear**. Called right-lateral or left-lateral depending on which way the opposite block seems to move."]]),
            IMG("f5gly2-horst.svg", alt="Two cross-sections: a horst where a block between two faults is higher than its neighbours and a graben where a block between two faults is lower", caption="Figure 2. A horst (left) and a graben (right)."),
            D("A **horst** is a block that is **pushed up** between two parallel faults. A **graben** is a block that has **dropped down** between two parallel faults. A series of grabens makes a rift valley.", title="Horst and graben"),
            H("Joints"),
            T("Joints are everywhere. They form when rock cools and shrinks (columnar joints in basalt), when pressure is released and when rock is stressed but does not move. Joints let water and roots into the rock, so they speed up weathering. They also control how a quarry is cut. Columns of rock formed by cooling and shrinkage cracks are called **columnar jointing**."),
            H("Unconformities"),
            T("Rock layers should lie one on another in an unbroken record, but often there are gaps in time. Uplift and erosion remove rock, and later deposition buries the eroded surface."),
            D("An **unconformity** is a surface between rock units that represents a **gap in the geological record**: a time when no deposition occurred, or rock was eroded. The surface is usually an old erosion surface.", title="Unconformity"),
            IMG("f5gly2-uncon.svg", alt="Three cross-sections in a row. Left: horizontal younger beds on tilted older beds, labelled angular. Middle: horizontal beds above and below an erosion surface, labelled parallel. Right: horizontal sediments above igneous or metamorphic rock, labelled heterolithic.", caption="Figure 3. Three kinds of unconformity. The amber line is the old erosion surface."),
            KEY("Three types of unconformity", "These are the definitions used in the Board's report.", cards=[
                ["Angular unconformity", "Younger **horizontal** beds lie on older **folded or tilted** beds."],
                ["Parallel unconformity", "Both the younger and older beds are horizontal, but they are separated by a plane of unconformity that is an erosion surface. (Many textbooks call this a disconformity.)"],
                ["Heterolithic unconformity", "An erosion surface that separates older **igneous or metamorphic** rocks from younger **sedimentary** rocks. (Many textbooks call this a nonconformity. Check your textbook.)"]]),
            EX("Worked example 1: type of fault", intro="In a quarry the layers on the right are lower than the same layers on the left. The fault plane dips to the right. What type of fault is it?", steps=[
                S("Find the hanging wall", text="The plane dips to the right, so the right-hand block lies above it: it is the hanging wall."),
                S("Direction of movement", text="The right-hand layers are lower, so the hanging wall has moved down."),
                S("Name", text="Hanging wall down: normal fault. This indicates tension."),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: how an unconformity forms", intro="Explain, in steps, how an angular unconformity forms.", steps=[
                S("Deposition", text="Horizontal beds are deposited."),
                S("Folding and uplift", text="Compression tilts or folds the beds and raises them above sea level."),
                S("Erosion", text="The tilted beds are worn down to a nearly flat erosion surface."),
                S("New deposition", text="The land sinks or the sea rises, and new horizontal beds are deposited on the erosion surface, which is the angular unconformity."),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Mixing up horst and graben", "A **horst** is the block that is **high**. A **graben** is the block that **dropped**. Graben sounds like grave: down."),
            MISTAKE("Not knowing the unconformity definitions", "The examiners reported that candidates could not give the definitions of the types of unconformity. Learn the three definitions above word for word, including what lies above and below."),
            QUIZ("In which kind of fault does the hanging wall move up relative to the footwall?", ["Normal fault", "Reverse fault", "Strike-slip fault", "Joint"], 1, "Hanging wall up = reverse fault, caused by compression.", hint="Normal is down. What is the other vertical type?", feedback=["In a normal fault the hanging wall moves down.", "", "A strike-slip fault moves sideways.", "A joint has no movement."]),
            QUIZ("Younger horizontal beds lie on older tilted beds. This is a", ["Parallel unconformity", "Heterolithic unconformity", "Angular unconformity", "Horst"], 2, "Horizontal on tilted is the angular unconformity.", hint="Look at the angle between the two sets of beds.", feedback=["In a parallel unconformity both sets are horizontal.", "A heterolithic unconformity has igneous or metamorphic rock below.", "", "A horst is a fault block."]),
            TIP("Faults, horst and graben, and the definitions of the three unconformities are favourite structured questions in Paper 2, and the diagrams are also used in Paper 1. The Board's examiners reported that candidates could not distinguish horst from graben or define the unconformities.", title="Exam link"),
            PQ("Differentiate between a horst and a graben.", "A horst is a block that has been pushed up between two parallel faults. A graben is a block that has dropped down between two parallel faults."),
            PQ("Define angular, parallel and heterolithic unconformities.", "Angular: younger horizontal beds lie on older folded or tilted beds. Parallel: older and younger beds are both horizontal but separated by an erosion surface. Heterolithic: an erosion surface separating older igneous or metamorphic rocks from younger sedimentary rocks."),
            PQ("State the type of stress that forms normal faults and the type that forms reverse faults.", "Normal faults form under tension; reverse faults form under compression."),
            SUM("A fault is a fracture with movement; a joint has none.",
                "Normal fault: hanging wall down (tension). Reverse fault: hanging wall up (compression). Strike-slip fault: sideways (shear).",
                "A horst is a raised block between two faults; a graben is a dropped block.",
                "Angular, parallel and heterolithic unconformities are old erosion surfaces that represent gaps in the record."),
        ],
    ),
    lesson(
        module=M5, topic="Principles of stratigraphy",
        title="Principles of stratigraphy and relative age",
        slug="gly2-stratigraphy-principles-relative-age",
        minutes=24,
        summary="Stratigraphy puts rocks in order of age. Superposition, original horizontality, lateral continuity, cross-cutting relationships, inclusions and faunal succession let you say which rock is older without knowing its age in years.",
        objectives=[
            "State the principles of superposition, original horizontality, lateral continuity, cross-cutting relationships, inclusions and faunal succession",
            "Work out the order of events from a cross-section",
            "Distinguish relative age from absolute age",
            "Explain how fossils help to correlate rocks in different places",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Stratigraphy", "Superposition", "Original horizontality", "Cross-cutting relationships", "Faunal succession", "Relative age", "Correlation"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("In a stack of newspapers on a shop counter, the oldest paper is at the bottom and the newest is on top. Nobody needs to read the dates to know this. Geologists use the same simple reasoning on rock layers. It lets them reconstruct events that happened millions of years before anyone was alive to record them."),
            D("**Stratigraphy** is the study of rock layers (**strata**): their order, age, composition and relationships. **Relative age** says whether one rock is older or younger than another. **Absolute age** gives an age in years.", title="Stratigraphy"),
            H("The principles"),
            KEY("Principles used to find relative age", "Learn each principle and one example.", cards=[
                ["Superposition", "In an undisturbed sequence of sedimentary layers, the oldest layer is at the bottom and the youngest at the top."],
                ["Original horizontality", "Sediments are deposited in nearly horizontal layers. If layers are tilted or folded, this happened after they were deposited."],
                ["Lateral continuity", "A layer extends sideways in all directions until it thins out or meets the edge of its basin. Layers on two sides of a valley were once joined."],
                ["Cross-cutting relationships", "A rock or feature that cuts across another is **younger** than the rock it cuts. This applies to faults, dykes and veins."],
                ["Inclusions", "Fragments of one rock inside another are older than the rock that contains them."],
                ["Faunal succession", "Fossil groups follow each other in a definite, known order through time, so a fossil tells the age of the rock that holds it."]]),
            H("Reading a cross-section"),
            IMG("f5gly2-strata.svg", alt="A cross-section with five horizontal layers labelled A at the bottom to E at the top. A vertical body F of igneous rock cuts layers A to D but does not enter E. An amber line marks an erosion surface between D and E.", caption="Figure 1. Which is older? Beds A to E and an igneous body F."),
            EX("Worked example 1: order of events", intro="Use Figure 1 to give the sequence of A, B, C, D, E and F from oldest to youngest.", steps=[
                S("Superposition for the beds", text="A is at the bottom, then B, C and D, with E on top. So the order of deposition is A, B, C, D, E."),
                S("Cross-cutting for F", text="F cuts through A, B, C and D, so F is younger than all four."),
                S("Does F cut E?", text="No, F stops at the erosion surface below E. So E was deposited after F was emplaced and after erosion had cut the top off F."),
                S("Answer", text="Oldest to youngest: A, B, C, D, F, E."),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: tilted beds", intro="Shale beds dip at 40 degrees and are overlain by horizontal sandstone beds. Which event came first, deposition of the shale or its tilting? Explain.", steps=[
                S("Original horizontality", text="The shale was deposited flat."),
                S("Cross-cutting relationship of the surface", text="The tilting took place after deposition of the shale, but before the sandstone was deposited, since the sandstone is not tilted."),
                S("Sequence", text="Shale deposited, shale tilted and eroded, sandstone deposited. The surface between them is an angular unconformity."),
            ]),
            H("Relative and absolute age"),
            T("The principles above give **relative** order. To give ages in years, geologists use **radiometric dating**, which uses the steady decay of radioactive elements. You will use it in the next lesson. A rate method can also be used for a rough estimate: if a layer is 12 m thick and sediment built up at a rate of 0.2 mm per year, the layer took 12 000 mm divided by 0.2 mm per year, which equals 60 000 years. These figures are invented for practice, and real rates vary a great deal."),
            H("Correlation and fossils"),
            D("**Correlation** is matching rock layers in different places and showing that they are the same age or the same unit. Geologists compare rock type, the order of layers and, most reliably, the fossils.", title="Correlation"),
            T("An **index fossil** is the remains of a type of organism that was widespread, lived for a short time and is easy to recognise. If the same index fossil is found in rocks in Douala and in rocks in another continent, those rocks are the same age."),
            MISTAKE("Saying a dyke is older than the rock it cuts", "A feature that cuts across a rock must be younger than the rock. A dyke cuts the beds, so the dyke is younger."),
            MISTAKE("Using superposition on overturned beds", "Superposition applies to beds in their original order. In an overturned fold the order of the beds is inverted, so check the way up using features such as graded bedding or ripple marks."),
            QUIZ("A granite pebble is found inside a sandstone bed. What is the relationship?", ["The granite is older than the sandstone", "The granite is younger than the sandstone", "They are the same age", "No conclusion is possible"], 0, "By inclusions, a fragment is older than the rock that holds it.", hint="The pebble had to exist before it was trapped in sand.", feedback=["", "The granite would have to be formed after the sandstone, but then it could not be an inclusion.", "They are not formed at the same time.", "The principle of inclusions gives a clear answer."]),
            QUIZ("Which principle says that a fault cutting a bed is younger than the bed?", ["Superposition", "Original horizontality", "Cross-cutting relationships", "Lateral continuity"], 2, "A feature that cuts across another is younger.", hint="Think of the word 'cut'.", feedback=["Superposition concerns the order of layers.", "Original horizontality concerns how layers were laid down.", "", "Lateral continuity concerns layers extending sideways."]),
            TIP("Relative-age exercises with a cross-section are common in Paper 1 and Paper 2. For each exercise, list the beds by superposition first, then place the dykes, faults and unconformities using cross-cutting relationships.", title="Exam link"),
            PQ("State the principle of superposition.", "In an undisturbed sequence of sedimentary layers, the oldest bed is at the bottom and the youngest at the top."),
            PQ("A fault cuts through beds P, Q and R and does not cut bed S, which lies above them. Give the order from oldest to youngest if P is lowest and R is just below S.", "P, Q, R, then the fault, then S. The fault is younger than P, Q and R, which it cuts, but older than S, which it does not cut."),
            PQ("What is faunal succession and why does it help to correlate rocks?", "Faunal succession is the idea that fossil groups follow each other in a definite order through time. A particular fossil therefore marks a particular age, so rocks in different places that contain it can be matched."),
            SUM("Stratigraphy puts rocks in order; relative age says older or younger, absolute age gives years.",
                "Superposition: oldest at the bottom. Original horizontality: layers began flat. Lateral continuity: layers extend sideways.",
                "Cross-cutting: a feature that cuts is younger. Inclusions: the fragment is older than the host.",
                "Faunal succession and index fossils let us correlate rocks across distances."),
        ],
    ),
    lesson(
        module=M5, topic="The geological time scale",
        title="The geological time scale and radiometric dating",
        slug="gly2-geological-time-scale-half-life",
        minutes=24,
        summary="The geological time scale divides 4.6 billion years into eons, eras and periods using the fossil record and radiometric dates. Half-life calculations give the age of a rock from how much of a radioactive parent isotope is left.",
        objectives=[
            "State the age of the Earth and name the eons, the three eras of the Phanerozoic and the periods in order",
            "Explain why the Precambrian is the longest part of Earth history",
            "Define half-life and calculate the age of a sample from the fraction of parent isotope left",
            "Choose a suitable isotope for dating a young or an old sample",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Geological time scale", "Era", "Period", "Precambrian", "Palaeozoic", "Mesozoic", "Cenozoic", "Half-life", "Radiometric dating"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("If the whole history of the Earth were squeezed into a single year, humans would appear only in the last few minutes of 31 December. Geologists divide that huge span into named blocks, the **geological time scale**, so that they can talk about events in order. The names come from the fossils in rocks, and the ages come from radiometric dating."),
            D("The **geological time scale** is the chronological table of Earth's history, divided from the largest units to the smallest into **eons**, **eras**, **periods** and **epochs**. The Earth is about **4.6 billion years** old.", title="Geological time scale"),
            KEY("Eons and eras", "Ages are approximate, in millions of years ago (Ma). Textbooks differ a little, so check yours.", cards=[
                ["Precambrian", "From the formation of the Earth (about 4 600 Ma) to about 539 Ma. It includes the Hadean, Archean and Proterozoic eons. The earliest life was simple, such as bacteria. Fossils are rare."],
                ["Palaeozoic era", "About 539 to 252 Ma. Marine invertebrates such as trilobites, then fishes, early land plants, amphibians and reptiles. Ends with the greatest mass extinction."],
                ["Mesozoic era", "About 252 to 66 Ma. The age of reptiles, with dinosaurs and ammonites. It ends with a mass extinction at the end of the Cretaceous."],
                ["Cenozoic era", "From about 66 Ma to the present. The age of mammals, and human beings appeared in its most recent part."]]),
            T("The last three eras form the **Phanerozoic** eon, the interval of obvious, abundant fossils. The word comes from Greek for 'visible life'."),
            KEY("The periods in order", "Learn these in order from oldest to youngest.", cards=[
                ["Palaeozoic", "Cambrian, Ordovician, Silurian, Devonian, Carboniferous, Permian"],
                ["Mesozoic", "Triassic, Jurassic, Cretaceous"],
                ["Cenozoic", "Palaeogene, Neogene, Quaternary (older books use Tertiary for Palaeogene plus Neogene)"]]),
            H("Radiometric dating and half-life"),
            D("The **half-life** of a radioactive isotope is the time taken for half of the atoms in a sample to decay. After one half-life, one half remains. After two, one quarter remains. After three, one eighth, and so on. The decay rate is fixed and is not changed by heat, pressure or chemical reactions.", title="Half-life"),
            F(r"\text{fraction left} = \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{n}, \quad \text{age} = n \times \text{half-life}", title="Using half-lives (n = number of half-lives)"),
            KEY("Choosing an isotope", "Use an isotope whose half-life is similar to the age of the sample.", cards=[
                ["Carbon-14", "Half-life about 5 730 years. Used for organic remains younger than about 50 000 years, such as wood, bone and charcoal."],
                ["Uranium-238 to lead-206", "Half-life about 4.5 billion years. Used for the oldest rocks and for the age of the Earth."],
                ["Potassium-40 to argon-40", "Half-life about 1.25 billion years. Used for igneous rocks and volcanic ash."]]),
            T("Radiometric dating works best on **igneous** rocks, whose crystals lock the isotopes when they cool. Sedimentary rocks contain grains of older rocks, so they are usually dated by the igneous layers above and below them, or by fossils."),
            EX("Worked example 1: carbon-14", intro="A piece of charcoal from an archaeological site contains one eighth (12.5%) of the carbon-14 that a living tree would have. The half-life of carbon-14 is 5 730 years. How old is the charcoal?", steps=[
                S("Number of half-lives", math=r"\frac{1}{8} = \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^3, \quad n = 3"),
                S("Age", math=r"3 \times 5\,730 = 17\,190 \text{ years}"),
            ], check=r"\left(\tfrac{1}{2}\right)^3 = 0.125"),
            EX("Worked example 2: an invented isotope", intro="A mineral contains an invented isotope with a half-life of 200 million years. A sample has 25% of its original parent atoms left. How old is it?", steps=[
                S("Find n", math=r"25\% = \frac{1}{4} = \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^2, \quad n = 2"),
                S("Age", math=r"2 \times 200 = 400 \text{ million years}"),
                S("Meaning", text="The rock crystallised about 400 million years ago, in the Palaeozoic era (invented figures for practice)."),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Thinking half the atoms means the age is half the half-life", "After one half-life, half is left. The age is the **number of half-lives** multiplied by the half-life, not half of anything."),
            MISTAKE("Using carbon-14 to date a rock", "Carbon-14 decays too quickly to date rocks millions of years old, and it is only found in once-living material. Use uranium or potassium isotopes for old rocks."),
            QUIZ("Which era comes immediately after the Palaeozoic?", ["Precambrian", "Mesozoic", "Cenozoic", "Quaternary"], 1, "The order is Precambrian, Palaeozoic, Mesozoic, Cenozoic.", hint="The order is Palaeo (old), Meso (middle), Ceno (recent).", feedback=["The Precambrian is before the Palaeozoic.", "", "The Cenozoic follows the Mesozoic.", "The Quaternary is a period, not an era."]),
            QUIZ("A sample has one quarter of its parent isotope left. How many half-lives have passed?", ["1", "2", "3", "4"], 1, "Half of half is a quarter, so two half-lives.", hint="Halve the amount twice: 1 to 1/2 to ...", feedback=["One half-life leaves one half.", "", "Three half-lives leave one eighth.", "Four half-lives leave one sixteenth."]),
            TIP("The time scale appears in Paper 1 questions asking you to put periods or eras in order, and half-life calculations are possible in both papers. Practise writing the periods without looking, and the half-life steps: fraction left, number of half-lives, then age.", title="Exam link"),
            PQ("State the approximate age of the Earth and name the three eras of the Phanerozoic in order.", "The Earth is about 4.6 billion years old. The eras are the Palaeozoic, Mesozoic and Cenozoic."),
            PQ("Define half-life.", "The half-life is the time taken for half of the atoms of a radioactive isotope in a sample to decay."),
            PQ("A rock contains one sixteenth of the parent atoms of an invented isotope with a half-life of 50 million years. How old is the rock?", "1/16 = (1/2)^4, so 4 half-lives have passed. Age = 4 x 50 = 200 million years (invented figures for practice)."),
            SUM("The Earth is about 4.6 billion years old; the Precambrian is the longest part.",
                "Eras: Palaeozoic, Mesozoic, Cenozoic. Periods run from Cambrian to Quaternary in the order you have learned.",
                "Half-life is the time for half the parent atoms to decay. Age = number of half-lives x half-life.",
                "Use carbon-14 for young organic remains and uranium or potassium isotopes for old rocks."),
        ],
    ),
    lesson(
        module=M5, topic="Fossils",
        title="Fossils: how they form and the major invertebrate groups",
        slug="gly2-fossils-invertebrate-groups",
        minutes=26,
        summary="Fossils are traces of ancient life preserved in rock. They form when hard parts are buried quickly. Trilobites, ammonites, brachiopods, bivalves, gastropods, corals and graptolites are identified by body shape, and each lived in a known time range, so they date rocks.",
        objectives=[
            "Define a fossil and explain the conditions and ways of fossilization",
            "Identify trilobites, ammonites, brachiopods, bivalves, gastropods, corals and graptolites from their features",
            "Distinguish a bivalve from a brachiopod",
            "Use the time ranges of fossils to date a rock and say what an index fossil is",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Fossil", "Fossilization", "Mould and cast", "Index fossil", "Trilobite", "Ammonite", "Brachiopod", "Bivalve", "Gastropod", "Coral", "Graptolite"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("A shell found in a quarry far from the sea is a puzzle. How did a sea animal reach a hillside, and why is it made of stone? The answer is that rocks that are now high above the sea were once seabed, and that the shell has been turned into rock. A fossil is a clue to the ancient environment and to the age of the rock."),
            D("A **fossil** is the preserved remains, or the traces, of a plant or animal that lived in the geological past, found in rocks.", title="Fossil"),
            H("How fossils form"),
            KEY("Conditions that favour fossilization", "Few organisms become fossils.", cards=[
                ["Hard parts", "Shells, bones and teeth last far longer than soft tissue."],
                ["Rapid burial", "Burial in mud or sand protects the remains from scavengers and from decay."],
                ["Little oxygen", "Without oxygen, bacteria cannot rot the remains quickly."],
                ["Later protection", "The rock must survive burial and uplift without being destroyed by heat or deformation."]]),
            KEY("Ways of preservation", "Preservation can be of the actual remains or of a copy.", cards=[
                ["Petrification (permineralisation, replacement)", "Minerals fill the pores of the hard part or replace its original mineral, turning it to stone."],
                ["Mould and cast", "A shell dissolves and leaves a hollow mould. If minerals fill the mould they form a cast with the shape of the outside of the shell."],
                ["Carbonisation", "Soft remains such as leaves leave a thin black film of carbon."],
                ["Original remains", "Very rare: insects in amber or animals frozen in ice."],
                ["Trace fossils", "Footprints, burrows and fossil droppings. They show how the animal lived but not its body shape."]]),
            H("Major invertebrate groups"),
            T("An **invertebrate** is an animal without a backbone. Most fossils in sedimentary rocks of the Palaeozoic and Mesozoic are marine invertebrates."),
            KEY("Trilobites and ammonites", "Both are index fossils.", cards=[
                ["Trilobite (arthropod)", "Body in three parts lengthways (an axial lobe and two side lobes) and three parts along its length: head (cephalon), thorax and tail (pygidium). Segmented body, with a hard outer skeleton. Lived in the sea from the **Cambrian to the Permian**, and are common in the Cambrian."],
                ["Ammonite (mollusc: cephalopod)", "A shell coiled in a flat spiral, divided into chambers by walls whose edges show as wavy lines (sutures) on the shell. Ribbed. Lived from the **Devonian to the Cretaceous** and are very useful in the Mesozoic."]]),
            KEY("Shelled animals with two shells", "Often confused.", cards=[
                ["Brachiopod", "Two valves of **different** sizes (an upper and a lower, often with a hole for a fleshy stalk). The plane of symmetry cuts through **both** valves, so each valve alone is symmetrical. Very common in the Palaeozoic."],
                ["Bivalve (mollusc)", "A **left** and a **right** valve, normally the **same size** (equivalved) and mirror images of each other. Each valve has an **umbo** (the raised, beak-like point near the hinge). Teeth (**dentition**) on the hinge, and **muscle scars** joined by a line called the **pallial line**. The plane of symmetry is along the line where the valves meet (the commissure). Cambrian or Ordovician to the present."]]),
            KEY("Other groups", "Learn one feature and the main range for each.", cards=[
                ["Gastropod (mollusc)", "A single shell coiled in a spiral, like a snail. Cambrian to the present."],
                ["Coral (cnidarian)", "Cone- or horn-shaped or colonial skeletons with radial walls (septa). Reef builders in warm shallow seas. Ordovician to the present."],
                ["Graptolite (colonial animals)", "Thin dark, saw-blade-like colonies on rock, looking like pencil marks. Floating in the sea. Ordovician and Silurian, and extinct by the Carboniferous."]]),
            T("Time ranges differ between textbooks by one period at either end, so check your textbook for the ranges your teacher uses."),
            D("An **index fossil** (zone fossil) is a fossil of an organism that was widespread, lived for a short time and is easy to recognise. It is used to date and correlate the rocks that contain it.", title="Index fossil"),
            EX("Worked example 1: identify", intro="Identify each fossil. (a) Spiral shell with wavy suture lines in chambers. (b) Two equal valves with umbones and a pallial line. (c) Saw-blade-like marks on a dark shale. (d) A body with a head shield, a segmented middle and a tail shield.", steps=[
                S("(a)", text="Ammonite."),
                S("(b)", text="Bivalve."),
                S("(c)", text="Graptolite."),
                S("(d)", text="Trilobite."),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: dating with overlapping ranges", intro="A rock layer contains both trilobites (Cambrian to Permian) and ammonites (Devonian to Cretaceous). Which periods could it belong to?", steps=[
                S("Find the overlap", text="Trilobites: Cambrian to Permian. Ammonites: Devonian to Cretaceous. Both occur together only from the Devonian to the Permian."),
                S("List the periods", text="Devonian, Carboniferous and Permian."),
                S("Meaning", text="The layer is Late Palaeozoic. More fossils with shorter ranges would narrow the age."),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Mixing up brachiopods and bivalves", "In a bivalve the two valves are the same size and the line of symmetry lies between them. In a brachiopod the valves are of different sizes and the line of symmetry runs through the middle of each valve."),
            MISTAKE("Thinking all organisms become fossils", "Most die and rot. Fossils need hard parts, fast burial and low oxygen, so the fossil record is incomplete."),
            QUIZ("Which fossil has a head shield, a segmented thorax and a tail shield?", ["Ammonite", "Trilobite", "Graptolite", "Gastropod"], 1, "That is the three-part body of a trilobite (cephalon, thorax, pygidium).", hint="Think 'tri' = three parts.", feedback=["An ammonite is a coiled shell.", "", "A graptolite looks like a saw-blade mark.", "A gastropod has a single spiral shell."]),
            QUIZ("Which is a feature of a bivalve, not of a brachiopod?", ["Two valves of unequal size", "Plane of symmetry through each valve", "Left and right valves that are mirror images", "Found only in the Cambrian"], 2, "Bivalves have equal left and right valves that mirror each other.", hint="Think of the shape of a mussel shell.", feedback=["Unequal valves are a brachiopod feature.", "That describes a brachiopod.", "", "Bivalves live from Cambrian or Ordovician to now."]),
            TIP("Fossil identification, fossilization and index fossils appear in Paper 1 and Paper 2. The Board's examiners reported that Question 6 on fossils was generally well answered but candidates failed to give the characteristics of bivalves, so learn the features listed above.", title="Exam link"),
            PQ("List the characteristics of a bivalve.", "Left and right valves of equal size, an umbo on each valve, a plane of symmetry along the commissure (the line between the valves), muscle scars joined by a pallial line and teeth (dentition) on the hinge."),
            PQ("State three conditions that favour fossilization.", "Hard parts, rapid burial and little oxygen, so that the remains do not decay or get eaten."),
            PQ("What is an index fossil and why is a trilobite not a good one for the Mesozoic?", "An index fossil is a widespread, easily recognised fossil of a species that lived for a short time, used to date rocks. Trilobites became extinct at the end of the Palaeozoic, so they cannot date Mesozoic rocks."),
            SUM("A fossil is preserved evidence of life in the geological past; hard parts and quick burial help.",
                "Preservation types: petrification, mould and cast, carbonisation, original remains and trace fossils.",
                "Trilobites, ammonites, brachiopods, bivalves, gastropods, corals and graptolites are recognised by their body plan.",
                "Index fossils have a short range and wide distribution and are used to date and correlate rocks."),
        ],
    ),
    lesson(
        module=M6, topic="The hydrologic cycle and groundwater",
        title="The hydrologic cycle, porosity, permeability and the water table",
        slug="gly2-hydrologic-cycle-groundwater",
        minutes=24,
        summary="Hydrogeology is the study of groundwater. Water cycles by evaporation, condensation, precipitation, runoff and infiltration. Porosity is the share of empty space in a rock; permeability is how easily water flows through it. The water table is the top of the saturated zone.",
        objectives=[
            "Describe the processes of the hydrologic cycle",
            "Define hydrogeology as the study of groundwater",
            "Define porosity and permeability, and calculate porosity",
            "Explain the water table and the zones above and below it",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Hydrologic cycle", "Hydrogeology", "Infiltration", "Porosity", "Permeability", "Water table", "Zone of saturation", "Zone of aeration"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("In many villages in Cameroon, drinking water comes from a well or a spring. That water fell as rain, soaked into the ground and moved slowly through rock. To find, protect and use it, you need to know how water moves through the Earth. This is **hydrogeology**."),
            D("**Hydrogeology** is the study of **groundwater**: its occurrence, movement and quality under the ground. It is not simply the study of water. (The examiners noted that many candidates defined it as 'the study of water', which is not enough.)", title="Hydrogeology"),
            H("The hydrologic cycle"),
            IMG("f5gly2-cycle.svg", alt="Diagram of the hydrologic cycle: evaporation from the sea, condensation in a cloud, precipitation, runoff down a slope, infiltration into the ground and groundwater flow back towards the sea", caption="Figure 1. The hydrologic cycle."),
            KEY("The hydrologic (water) cycle", "Water moves between the sea, air and land. The Sun's heat drives it.", cards=[
                ["Evaporation", "Liquid water becomes vapour from seas, lakes and rivers. Plants also release vapour (transpiration)."],
                ["Condensation", "Vapour cools and forms tiny droplets, making clouds."],
                ["Precipitation", "Water falls as rain, hail or snow."],
                ["Runoff", "Water flows over the ground surface into streams and rivers."],
                ["Infiltration", "Water soaks into the soil and rock and becomes groundwater, which flows slowly back to rivers and the sea."]]),
            H("Porosity and permeability"),
            D("**Porosity** is the percentage of the total volume of a rock that is **empty space** (pores). It says how much water a rock can hold. **Permeability** is the ability of a rock to let fluids **flow through** it. It depends on how well the pores are connected.", title="Porosity and permeability"),
            F(r"\text{porosity} (\%) = \frac{\text{volume of pores}}{\text{total volume of rock}} \times 100", title="Porosity"),
            T("Rocks with high porosity can still have low permeability. Clay has very small pores, so it can hold a lot of water, but the water barely moves. Sand and gravel, with large connected pores, are both porous and permeable. Fractured rock can be permeable even if it has few pores. A rock that does not let water pass is **impermeable**, and one that does is **permeable**."),
            KEY("Some comparisons", "Common examples; check your textbook for typical values.", cards=[
                ["Sand, gravel, sandstone", "Porous and permeable: good for storing and carrying water."],
                ["Clay, shale", "Porous but impermeable: holds water but does not let it flow."],
                ["Unfractured granite", "Low porosity and low permeability. Water is found only in cracks and weathered zones."]]),
            H("The water table"),
            D("The **water table** is the upper surface of the zone below the ground in which all the pores are full of water. Below it is the **zone of saturation**. Above it is the **zone of aeration** (unsaturated zone), where pores contain both air and water.", title="Water table"),
            T("The water table is not flat. It usually follows the shape of the land, higher under hills and lower in valleys. It rises after the rainy season and falls in the dry season, so a shallow well may dry up in the dry season. Where the water table meets the ground surface, water flows out as a spring or fills a lake or river."),
            EX("Worked example 1: porosity", intro="A 2 000 cm3 block of sandstone is saturated. Draining it releases 440 cm3 of water. What is its porosity?", steps=[
                S("Volume of pores", text="The water that drained out filled the pores: 440 cm3 (assuming all pores drained)."),
                S("Porosity", math=r"\frac{440}{2000} \times 100 = 22\%"),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: choose the best well site", intro="A village can dig in clay, in coarse sand or in unfractured granite. Where is the best place for a well and why?", steps=[
                S("Clay", text="Holds water but is impermeable, so water would enter the well too slowly."),
                S("Granite", text="Has very few pores or cracks to store water."),
                S("Sand", text="Porous and permeable: it stores water and lets it flow into the well. Sand is the best choice (below the water table)."),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Defining hydrogeology as the study of water", "Hydrogeology is the study of **groundwater**. The study of all water, on and above the ground, is hydrology."),
            MISTAKE("Saying porous means permeable", "Porosity is storage, permeability is flow. Clay is porous but not permeable."),
            QUIZ("What is porosity?", ["How quickly water flows through a rock", "The percentage of a rock that is empty space", "The depth of the water table", "The hardness of a rock"], 1, "Porosity is the percentage of the volume of a rock that is pore space.", hint="It is about storage.", feedback=["That is permeability.", "", "That is not porosity.", "Hardness is a different property."]),
            QUIZ("Which process puts water into the ground?", ["Evaporation", "Condensation", "Infiltration", "Transpiration"], 2, "Infiltration is the soaking of water into the soil and rock.", hint="Think of rain soaking into a field.", feedback=["Evaporation returns water to the air.", "Condensation makes clouds.", "", "Transpiration is release of vapour by plants."]),
            TIP("Hydrogeology questions appear in Paper 1 and Paper 2. The Board's examiners reported that the definition of hydrogeology was a weakness, since many candidates wrote 'study of water' instead of 'study of groundwater'.", title="Exam link"),
            PQ("Define hydrogeology.", "Hydrogeology is the study of groundwater, its occurrence, movement and quality."),
            PQ("Differentiate between porosity and permeability.", "Porosity is the percentage of a rock that is pore space, so it shows storage. Permeability is the ability of the rock to let fluids flow through, which depends on connected pores."),
            PQ("A 500 cm3 sample of sand has pores totalling 175 cm3. Calculate its porosity.", "175 / 500 x 100 = 35 per cent."),
            SUM("Hydrogeology is the study of groundwater.",
                "The water cycle: evaporation, condensation, precipitation, runoff and infiltration.",
                "Porosity = volume of pores / total volume x 100; permeability is how easily fluid flows.",
                "The water table is the top of the zone of saturation and follows the land surface."),
        ],
    ),
    lesson(
        module=M6, topic="Aquifers, springs and wells",
        title="Aquifers, springs, wells and artesian water",
        slug="gly2-aquifers-springs-wells",
        minutes=24,
        summary="An aquifer is a rock that stores and yields water. An unconfined aquifer has a water table; a confined aquifer lies between impermeable layers and is under pressure. Springs occur where the water table meets the surface, and artesian wells tap pressurised water that rises by itself.",
        objectives=[
            "Define aquifer and aquiclude (impermeable layer) and give examples",
            "Distinguish unconfined from confined aquifers",
            "Explain how a spring and an artesian well form, with a labelled diagram",
            "Describe the effects of over-pumping and pollution on a well",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Aquifer", "Aquiclude", "Unconfined aquifer", "Confined aquifer", "Spring", "Artesian well", "Recharge area", "Cone of depression"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("Many towns and villages in Cameroon now depend on boreholes. A drilling team cannot choose just any spot. They need rock that holds a lot of water and lets it flow to the borehole. In some places the water rises by itself from the ground. Understanding aquifers explains all of this."),
            D("An **aquifer** is a body of rock or sediment that is both **porous and permeable**, so that it stores water and yields useful amounts to wells and springs. An **aquiclude** (or impermeable layer) is a rock, such as clay or shale, that does not transmit water.", title="Aquifer"),
            KEY("Two types of aquifer", "Whether there is an impermeable layer on top decides the type.", cards=[
                ["Unconfined aquifer", "The top is the water table. There is no impermeable layer above, so rain soaks in directly from above. The water is at atmospheric pressure. A well fills only to the level of the water table."],
                ["Confined aquifer", "An aquifer sandwiched between two impermeable layers. The water is **under pressure** because it is fed from a higher recharge area. A well drilled into it can fill to a level above the top of the aquifer."]]),
            IMG("f5gly2-unconfined.svg", alt="Cross-section showing a water table below the ground surface, a dug well reaching below it, a spring where the water table meets the surface, the zone of saturation and an impermeable clay layer below", caption="Figure 1. Water table, well and spring in an unconfined aquifer."),
            H("Springs"),
            D("A **spring** is a place where groundwater flows naturally out onto the surface. It forms where the water table meets the ground surface, for example at the foot of a slope, or where a permeable layer lies on top of an impermeable one, or where a fault or fracture lets water escape.", title="Spring"),
            H("Wells and artesian water"),
            D("A **well** is a hole dug or drilled down to the water table or into an aquifer. A **borehole** is a narrow drilled well, usually with a pump. An **artesian well** is a well in a **confined aquifer** in which the water rises above the top of the aquifer. If the pressure surface is above the ground, the water flows out without a pump.", title="Wells"),
            IMG("f5gly2-artesian.svg", alt="Cross-section of a sloping aquifer between two clay layers with the recharge area on high ground at the left, a dashed pressure surface line sloping above the ground on the right, and an artesian well with water spurting", caption="Figure 2. An artesian well. The dashed line is the pressure (potentiometric) surface."),
            KEY("What an artesian system needs", "Four conditions are required.", cards=[
                ["A permeable aquifer", "Usually sandstone or gravel."],
                ["Impermeable layers above and below", "They confine the water."],
                ["A recharge area at a higher level", "Rain soaks in on high ground where the aquifer comes to the surface."],
                ["Tilted or folded beds", "The aquifer dips down, so water at depth is under the pressure of the water column."]]),
            H("Using groundwater wisely"),
            T("When a well is pumped, the water table around it drops in the shape of a cone, called the **cone of depression**. If too many wells pump from the same aquifer, or water is taken faster than rain refills it, the water table falls, shallow wells dry up and the quality can get worse. Latrines, rubbish dumps and fuel stores can pollute groundwater, so wells should be placed uphill from them and as far away as local health guidance says. Check the current rules with your local water or health office."),
            EX("Worked example 1: explain an artesian well", intro="Explain why water rises in an artesian well without a pump.", steps=[
                S("Recharge area", text="Rain enters the aquifer on high ground where it reaches the surface."),
                S("Confinement", text="Impermeable clay above and below traps the water in the aquifer as it dips deeper."),
                S("Pressure", text="Water at depth is under pressure from the water higher up in the aquifer, so it rises in the well, up to the pressure surface. If that surface is above the ground, it flows out."),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: a borehole supply", intro="A borehole yields 1.2 cubic metres of water per hour. It is pumped for 10 hours a day. (1 cubic metre = 1 000 litres.) How many people can it supply if each person needs 20 litres a day?", steps=[
                S("Daily volume", math=r"1.2 \times 10 = 12 \text{ m}^3 = 12\,000 \text{ litres}"),
                S("People supplied", math=r"\frac{12\,000}{20} = 600"),
                S("Answer", text="The borehole can supply about 600 people (invented figures for practice)."),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Saying artesian means 'deep'", "A well is artesian because the water in a confined aquifer is under pressure and rises above the aquifer, not because it is deep."),
            MISTAKE("Forgetting the confining layer", "Without impermeable layers above and below, the water is not confined, and there is no pressure."),
            QUIZ("What does a confined aquifer have that an unconfined aquifer does not?", ["A water table", "An impermeable layer above it", "Pores", "Rainwater"], 1, "A confined aquifer has an impermeable layer above and below.", hint="The word 'confined' means shut in.", feedback=["The water table belongs to an unconfined aquifer.", "", "Both types of aquifer have pores.", "Both are fed by rain."]),
            QUIZ("A spring forms where", ["The water table meets the ground surface", "The rock is dry", "The water table is deep underground", "A river flows into the sea"], 0, "A spring appears where the water table meets the surface.", hint="Where does groundwater come out?", feedback=["", "A dry rock has no water.", "A deep water table is the opposite of a spring.", "A river mouth is not a spring."]),
            TIP("Aquifers, springs and artesian wells are tested in Paper 1 and as a labelled diagram question in Paper 2. Learn the sketch of an artesian system with recharge area, aquifer, impermeable layers and pressure surface.", title="Exam link"),
            PQ("Define an aquifer and give one example of a rock that makes a good aquifer.", "An aquifer is a porous, permeable rock body that stores and yields useful amounts of water. Sandstone, sand or gravel are good examples."),
            PQ("Explain the formation of a spring.", "A spring forms where the water table meets the ground surface, or where permeable rock lies on impermeable rock, or where a fault lets water escape. Groundwater then flows out of the ground."),
            PQ("State two effects of pumping too much water from a borehole.", "The water table falls and a cone of depression forms. Shallow wells nearby dry up and water quality may worsen."),
            SUM("An aquifer is a porous, permeable rock that stores and yields water; an impermeable layer is an aquiclude.",
                "Unconfined aquifers have a water table; confined aquifers lie between impermeable layers under pressure.",
                "A spring forms where the water table meets the ground; an artesian well taps confined water that rises above the aquifer.",
                "Over-pumping and pollution reduce the supply and quality of groundwater."),
        ],
    ),
    lesson(
        module=M6, topic="Petroleum, natural gas and coal",
        title="Petroleum, natural gas and coal: origin and occurrence",
        slug="gly2-petroleum-gas-coal",
        minutes=24,
        summary="Petroleum and gas form from buried marine organisms, move from a source rock to a porous reservoir and are held by a cap rock in a trap. Coal forms from buried swamp plants: peat, lignite, bituminous coal, anthracite. Cameroon's oil comes from coastal basins such as Rio del Rey.",
        objectives=[
            "Explain how petroleum and natural gas form from organic matter",
            "Name and describe the source rock, reservoir rock, cap rock and trap",
            "Describe the stages in the formation of coal from peat to anthracite",
            "State where Cameroon's petroleum basins are, with hedges, and say why fossil fuels matter",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Petroleum", "Natural gas", "Source rock", "Reservoir rock", "Cap rock", "Trap", "Migration", "Peat", "Lignite", "Bituminous coal", "Anthracite"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("Fuel for cars and generators, gas for cooking and tar for roads all come from rocks. Oil and gas are produced in Cameroon, mostly offshore, and are among the country's important resources. Geologists must find where the oil formed, how it travelled and where it was trapped. This lesson explains that story."),
            D("**Petroleum** (crude oil) is a mixture of liquid hydrocarbons found in sedimentary rocks. **Natural gas** is a mixture of lighter hydrocarbons, mainly **methane**, usually found with oil or on its own. **Coal** is a black or brown combustible sedimentary rock made mainly of carbon from plants.", title="Fossil fuels"),
            D("A **hydrocarbon** is a compound made only of carbon and hydrogen. Oil, gas and coal are called **fossil fuels** because they come from the remains of living things that were buried millions of years ago.", title="Hydrocarbon and fossil fuel"),
            H("How petroleum and gas form and collect"),
            KEY("Four steps", "Follow the oil from origin to trap.", cards=[
                ["1. Source", "Dead plankton and algae sink to the floor of a sea or lake and are buried in fine mud with little oxygen. The mud becomes organic-rich shale, the **source rock**."],
                ["2. Cooking", "As the mud is buried deeper, heat and pressure change the organic matter into oil and gas. Too little heat gives no oil; too much destroys it."],
                ["3. Migration", "Oil and gas are lighter than water, so they move upward and sideways through permeable rock until they are stopped."],
                ["4. Trapping", "They collect in a **reservoir rock** (porous and permeable, such as sandstone or limestone) beneath an impermeable **cap rock** (such as shale or salt). The whole arrangement is a **trap**."]]),
            IMG("f5gly2-trap.svg", alt="Cross-section of an anticline showing a source rock at the bottom, a reservoir rock above it holding water, oil and gas in layers, and a grey cap rock on top, with a well drilled through the crest", caption="Figure 1. An anticline trap. Gas is lightest and collects at the top, then oil, then water."),
            T("In a trap the fluids separate by weight: **gas** on top, **oil** in the middle and **water** at the bottom. Common traps are **anticlines** (as in Figure 1), **fault traps** where a fault places an impermeable bed against the reservoir, **salt domes** and **stratigraphic traps** where a reservoir bed thins out and is sealed by clay."),
            H("Coal"),
            T("Coal forms from land plants that grow in swamps. Dead plants pile up in wet, oxygen-poor conditions and do not rot completely, forming **peat**. If peat is buried under sediment, rising pressure and temperature squeeze out water and raise the proportion of carbon. The result is a series called the **rank** of the coal."),
            KEY("From peat to anthracite", "Each step needs more burial, heat and pressure and gives a higher carbon content and a more energy-rich coal.", cards=[
                ["Peat", "Soft, brown, plant remains still visible, a lot of water."],
                ["Lignite", "Brown, soft coal; low carbon."],
                ["Bituminous coal", "Black, banded, widely used coal."],
                ["Anthracite", "Hard, shiny black coal with the highest carbon content and the most heat."]]),
            H("Petroleum in Cameroon"),
            T("Cameroon's oil is produced from sedimentary basins on the coast. Rio del Rey basin in the south-west, offshore, has long been an important source, and the Douala and Kribi-Campo basins are also known for oil and gas. Check your textbook for the names and the locations of fields; the production figures change every year, so look for current figures from official sources instead of memorising numbers. Coal is not a major Cameroonian resource, so use your textbook for examples of coal fields."),
            EX("Worked example 1: name the parts", intro="Use Figure 1. A well is drilled at the crest of the anticline. State what the grey layer, the yellow layer holding fluids and the dark lower layer are, and why the oil does not escape.", steps=[
                S("Grey layer on top", text="Cap rock, impermeable (for example shale)."),
                S("Layer holding the fluids", text="Reservoir rock, porous and permeable (for example sandstone)."),
                S("Dark layer at the base", text="Source rock, rich in organic matter."),
                S("Why oil stays", text="Oil rises, but the impermeable cap rock stops it, and the folded shape (an anticline) collects it at the crest."),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: how much oil?", intro="A reservoir covers 2 square kilometres and is 30 m thick. Its porosity is 20% and 70% of the pore space is filled with oil. How many cubic metres of oil does it contain? (Invented figures for practice.)", steps=[
                S("Volume of rock", math=r"2\,000\,000 \text{ m}^2 \times 30 \text{ m} = 60\,000\,000 \text{ m}^3"),
                S("Pore volume", math=r"60\,000\,000 \times 0.20 = 12\,000\,000 \text{ m}^3"),
                S("Oil volume", math=r"12\,000\,000 \times 0.70 = 8\,400\,000 \text{ m}^3"),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Saying oil is found in an underground lake", "Oil is held in the tiny pores of a reservoir rock such as sandstone. It is not a cave filled with oil."),
            MISTAKE("Mixing up source rock and reservoir rock", "The source rock is where the oil forms (organic-rich shale). The reservoir rock is where the oil collects (porous, permeable rock)."),
            QUIZ("Which rock acts as the cap rock in an oil trap?", ["Porous sandstone", "Impermeable shale", "Fractured limestone", "Loose gravel"], 1, "A cap rock must be impermeable so that oil cannot pass through.", hint="It must stop the oil rising.", feedback=["Porous sandstone makes a reservoir rock.", "", "Fractured limestone lets fluids pass.", "Gravel is permeable."]),
            QUIZ("Which is the correct order of coal rank from lowest to highest?", ["Anthracite, bituminous coal, lignite, peat", "Peat, lignite, bituminous coal, anthracite", "Lignite, peat, anthracite, bituminous coal", "Peat, bituminous coal, lignite, anthracite"], 1, "Rank increases with burial: peat, lignite, bituminous coal, anthracite.", hint="Begin with the unaltered plant material.", feedback=["That is the order from highest to lowest.", "", "Lignite comes after peat.", "Bituminous coal comes after lignite."]),
            TIP("The origin of oil and gas, the parts of a trap and the stages of coal formation are tested in Paper 1 and in structured questions in Paper 2. The Rio del Rey and Douala basins appear in the blueprint, so know their names, and check the details in your textbook.", title="Exam link"),
            PQ("Describe how petroleum forms and collects in a reservoir.", "Dead marine plankton are buried in fine mud with little oxygen. Heat and pressure convert the organic matter to oil and gas in the source rock. The lighter fluids migrate upward into a porous reservoir rock and are stopped by an impermeable cap rock, collecting in a trap such as an anticline."),
            PQ("Name the stages in the formation of coal in order.", "Peat, lignite, bituminous coal, anthracite."),
            PQ("Why do gas, oil and water separate in a reservoir and in what order do they lie from top to bottom?", "They separate by density. Gas is lightest and lies on top, oil lies below it and water, the densest, lies at the bottom."),
            SUM("Petroleum and gas form from buried marine organisms in a source rock and migrate to a reservoir.",
                "A trap has a reservoir rock, a cap rock and a closed shape such as an anticline. In it gas lies over oil, over water.",
                "Coal forms from buried swamp plants: peat, lignite, bituminous coal, anthracite.",
                "Cameroon's petroleum comes mainly from coastal basins such as Rio del Rey and Douala (check your textbook)."),
        ],
    ),
    lesson(
        module=M6, topic="Ore deposits and mineral resources of Cameroon",
        title="Ore deposits and Cameroon's mineral resources",
        slug="gly2-ore-deposits-cameroon-resources",
        minutes=24,
        summary="An ore is a rock holding a metal that can be mined at a profit. It contains ore minerals and waste gangue; its metal content is the tenor. Ores form by magmatic, hydrothermal, placer, residual and sedimentary processes. Cameroon has bauxite, gold, iron ore, rutile and limestone.",
        objectives=[
            "Define ore, ore mineral, gangue and tenor as the Board's report does",
            "Describe five ways in which ore deposits form",
            "Name Cameroon's main mineral resources and the way each forms, with hedged localities",
            "Decide from tenor, price and cost whether a deposit is an ore",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Ore", "Ore mineral", "Gangue", "Tenor", "Hydrothermal", "Placer", "Residual deposit", "Bauxite"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("Aluminium cooking pots, iron roofing sheets, cement for building and gold jewellery all start in rock. Cameroon has deposits of bauxite, gold, iron ore, rutile and limestone, among others. Some are mined, some are only explored, and some are dug by small-scale miners. To judge a deposit, geologists need a few key terms."),
            D("An **ore** is a natural rock or mineral deposit that contains a **metal** (or other useful substance) in high enough amounts that it can be **mined at a profit**. An **ore mineral** is the mineral in the ore from which the metal is extracted. **Gangue** is the unwanted mineral (and rock) found in an ore along with the ore mineral. **Tenor** is the metal content of an ore, for example grams of gold per tonne of rock.", title="Ore, ore mineral, gangue, tenor"),
            T("Whether a rock is an ore changes with **price** (higher prices make poorer rocks worth mining), **tenor**, **cost** of mining and transport, and the **amount** of ore."),
            H("How ore deposits form"),
            KEY("Main processes", "Each concentrates a metal in one place.", cards=[
                ["Magmatic", "Heavy ore minerals sink and gather in the lower part of a cooling magma chamber (for example chromite)."],
                ["Hydrothermal", "Hot, mineral-rich water moves through cracks and deposits ore minerals as **veins** when it cools. Many gold, copper and lead veins form this way."],
                ["Placer", "Heavy, hard and unreactive minerals, such as gold and rutile, are weathered out of rock and concentrated in river gravels or beach sands, where they settle first because they are dense."],
                ["Residual (weathering)", "In hot, wet climates, heavy rain leaches soluble material from a rock and leaves behind a residue rich in aluminium (bauxite) or iron."],
                ["Sedimentary", "Ore minerals are deposited in layers from water, as in banded iron formations or in limestone."]]),
            H("Cameroon's minerals"),
            T("The list below names the commonly cited resources. The exact localities, the mining status and the figures change with time, so check your textbook for the localities and look for official, current information instead of memorising production numbers."),
            KEY("Resources named in the syllabus blueprint", "Each with its use and a usual way of formation.", cards=[
                ["Bauxite", "Ore of **aluminium**. Forms by intense weathering of aluminium-rich rocks in a hot, wet climate (residual deposit). Reported in the Adamawa region and elsewhere: check your textbook."],
                ["Gold", "Found in hydrothermal quartz veins and in **placer** deposits in rivers. Alluvial gold is often worked by small-scale miners, for example in the east of the country: check your textbook."],
                ["Iron ore", "Source of iron and steel. Many large deposits are banded iron formations or weathered iron-rich rocks. Large deposits are reported in the south-east: check your textbook."],
                ["Rutile", "A titanium oxide, ore of **titanium**, which is very hard and resists weathering, so it collects in sands and gravels. Reported in the Akonolinga area: check your textbook."],
                ["Limestone", "Calcium carbonate rock used to make **cement** and lime. It is a sedimentary rock formed from shells and chemical precipitation. Reported in the north (for example Figuil): check your textbook."]]),
            EX("Worked example 1: is it an ore?", intro="A gold deposit has a tenor of 5 grams of gold per tonne. Gold is worth FCFA 30 000 per gram (an invented price for practice). Mining and processing cost FCFA 120 000 per tonne. Is the deposit an ore? What if the cost were FCFA 200 000 per tonne?", steps=[
                S("Value of one tonne", math=r"5 \times 30\,000 = 150\,000 \text{ FCFA}"),
                S("Cost 120 000", text="Value 150 000 is above the cost 120 000, so there is a profit of 30 000 FCFA per tonne. It is an ore."),
                S("Cost 200 000", text="Value 150 000 is below the cost 200 000, so there would be a loss. It would not be an ore under those conditions."),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: how much metal?", intro="An ore contains 5 grams of gold per tonne. How much gold is in 200 tonnes of ore?", steps=[
                S("Multiply", math=r"200 \times 5 = 1\,000 \text{ grams}"),
                S("Convert", text="1 000 grams is 1 kilogram of gold."),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Defining tenor as the amount of the ore body", "Tenor is the **metal content**, the percentage or the grams per tonne, not the size of the deposit."),
            MISTAKE("Calling any rock with metal an ore", "An ore must be **mined at a profit**. A rock with a few grams of metal in a remote place may not be an ore."),
            QUIZ("What is gangue?", ["The metal in an ore", "The unwanted mineral in an ore", "The rock that overlies an ore", "A tool for mining"], 1, "Gangue is the worthless mineral that comes with the ore mineral.", hint="It is the part you throw away.", feedback=["The metal is what is wanted.", "", "That is overburden.", "Gangue is a mineral, not a tool."]),
            QUIZ("Which deposit is formed by intense tropical weathering that leaves aluminium behind?", ["Bauxite", "Gold in quartz veins", "Limestone", "Rutile in a magma chamber"], 0, "Bauxite is a residual deposit formed by deep leaching in a hot, wet climate.", hint="Which ore is an aluminium ore?", feedback=["", "Vein gold is hydrothermal.", "Limestone is a sedimentary rock.", "Rutile concentrates in placers."]),
            TIP("The Board's examiners reported that candidates could not define gangue mineral, mineral tenor or ore mineral in a structured question on economic geology. Learn the three definitions above exactly. Cameroonian deposits can be asked in Paper 1; check the localities in your textbook.", title="Exam link"),
            PQ("Define ore mineral, gangue mineral and mineral tenor.", "An ore mineral is a mineral deposit that contains a metal and can be mined at a profit. Gangue is the unwanted mineral found in an ore. Tenor is the metal content of an ore."),
            PQ("Explain how a placer gold deposit forms.", "Gold is weathered out of rock. Because gold is dense and unreactive, it is carried by rivers but settles quickly where the flow slows, concentrating in gravels and sands."),
            PQ("Name two uses of limestone and say how limestone forms.", "Cement manufacture and agricultural lime or building stone. It forms from shells and by chemical precipitation of calcium carbonate in water (a sedimentary rock)."),
            SUM("An ore can be mined at a profit; an ore mineral holds the metal; gangue is waste; tenor is metal content.",
                "Ores form by magmatic, hydrothermal, placer, residual and sedimentary processes.",
                "Cameroon's bauxite, gold, iron ore, rutile and limestone are named in the blueprint; check your textbook for localities.",
                "Price, tenor and cost decide whether a deposit is an ore."),
        ],
    ),
    lesson(
        module=M6, topic="Geohazards",
        title="Geohazards: earthquakes, volcanoes, landslides and the Lake Nyos and Monoun disasters",
        slug="gly2-geohazards-earthquakes-volcanoes-lakes",
        minutes=26,
        summary="Geohazards are natural processes that can harm people. Earthquakes, volcanic eruptions and landslides are hazards; so are the gas releases at Lake Monoun (1984) and Lake Nyos (1986), where carbon dioxide from the lake bottom killed people. Monitoring, planning and education reduce the risk.",
        objectives=[
            "Define hazard, risk and disaster",
            "Describe the hazards of earthquakes and volcanic eruptions and how to reduce them",
            "Explain the cause and effects of the Lake Nyos and Lake Monoun gas disasters and how they are managed",
            "Compare earthquake magnitudes and find the distance of an earthquake from the S-P lag",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Geohazard", "Risk", "Earthquake", "Epicentre", "Magnitude", "Volcanic hazard", "Landslide", "Limnic eruption", "Degassing"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("Cameroon is not a country with frequent large earthquakes, but it has an active volcano, Mount Cameroon, steep hills prone to landslides, and two crater lakes that have killed people with an invisible gas. Geohazards are not only events in faraway countries. This lesson explains the main geohazards and how people can reduce the danger."),
            D("A **geohazard** (natural geological hazard) is a geological process or event that can cause harm to people or property. **Risk** is the chance that harm will occur: it depends on the **hazard**, on **how many people and how much property are exposed** and on how **vulnerable** they are. A **disaster** is what happens when a hazard seriously harms a community.", title="Hazard, risk and disaster"),
            H("Earthquakes"),
            D("An **earthquake** is shaking of the ground caused by a sudden release of energy, usually movement along a fault. The point underground where it starts is the **focus**. The point on the surface directly above is the **epicentre**. The **magnitude** measures the energy released.", title="Earthquake"),
            T("Most harm comes from **ground shaking** that collapses poorly built houses. Other hazards are **landslides** triggered by shaking, **liquefaction** (wet sandy ground behaves like liquid), **fires** from broken gas and electric lines and **tsunamis** where the sea floor is moved. Each increase of one unit of magnitude means ground motion about **10 times** larger and about **32 times** more energy released."),
            H("Volcanic hazards"),
            KEY("Main volcanic hazards", "Different hazards reach different distances.", cards=[
                ["Lava flows", "Slow enough to walk away from but can destroy buildings and farmland."],
                ["Ash fall", "Collapses roofs, harms lungs and crops and affects air travel."],
                ["Pyroclastic flows", "Fast, hot clouds of gas and ash. Extremely dangerous."],
                ["Lahars (mudflows)", "Volcanic ash and debris mixed with rain or melted ice flow down valleys."],
                ["Gases", "Carbon dioxide, sulphur dioxide and others can poison people and animals."]]),
            T("Mount Cameroon is an active volcano and has erupted several times in recent decades. Its eruptions are mostly lava flows and ash, and so people living on its slopes must have warnings and evacuation plans. Check current information from the national volcano observatory for details."),
            H("Landslides"),
            T("Landslides are the form of mass wasting that you studied in the earlier lesson. They are triggered by heavy rain, earthquakes and human activity such as cutting steep slopes or removing trees. People living on steep hillsides in towns such as Bamenda, Limbe and Douala suburbs may be at risk during the rainy season."),
            H("The gas disasters of Lakes Monoun and Nyos"),
            T("Lake Monoun and Lake Nyos are crater lakes in the north-west and west of Cameroon, on the Cameroon Volcanic Line. In **August 1984** a sudden release of gas from Lake Monoun killed about 37 people. In **August 1986** a much larger release from Lake Nyos killed about 1 700 people, as well as many cattle and other animals. Figures differ a little between sources, so check your textbook."),
            D("A **limnic eruption** is the sudden release of dissolved gas, mainly **carbon dioxide**, from the deep water of a lake. Volcanic carbon dioxide seeps into the bottom water of the lake from below. The water at the bottom becomes saturated. A trigger, such as a landslide, a strong wind or a change in water temperature, can make the water turn over, and the gas bubbles out as a cloud.", title="Limnic eruption"),
            T("Carbon dioxide is **colourless and has no smell** and is **heavier than air**. The cloud flowed down the valleys and filled low ground. People and animals suffocated because the cloud displaced the oxygen. After these events, scientists have studied the lakes and installed **degassing pipes** that let the gas from the lake bottom escape slowly and safely. Check current information on the work at each lake."),
            KEY("Reducing risk", "People cannot stop geohazards but they can lower the risk.", cards=[
                ["Monitoring and warning", "Seismographs, volcano observatories and gas sensors give early warning."],
                ["Land-use planning", "Do not build on steep slopes, floodplains or in valleys below a gassy lake."],
                ["Strong buildings", "Follow building codes to withstand shaking."],
                ["Education and drills", "People who know the warning signs and escape routes survive more often."],
                ["Engineering", "Degassing pipes in the crater lakes, drainage and retaining walls on slopes."]]),
            EX("Worked example 1: comparing magnitudes", intro="How many times more energy is released by a magnitude 7 earthquake than by a magnitude 5? (Use 32 times per unit as a round figure.)", steps=[
                S("Difference in magnitude", text="7 minus 5 is 2 units."),
                S("Energy ratio", math=r"32 \times 32 = 1\,024 \approx 1\,000"),
                S("Answer", text="About 1 000 times more energy. (The exact factor per unit is 10 to the power 1.5, about 31.6, which gives exactly 1 000 for two units.)"),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: distance from the S-P lag", intro="P waves travel at 6.0 km/s and S waves at 3.5 km/s (invented values). A seismograph records the S wave 10 seconds after the P wave. How far away is the earthquake?", steps=[
                S("Let the distance be d", text="Travel time of S minus travel time of P equals 10 s."),
                S("Equation", math=r"\frac{d}{3.5} - \frac{d}{6.0} = 10"),
                S("Solve", math=r"d \left(\frac{1}{3.5} - \frac{1}{6.0}\right) = 10 \Rightarrow d \times 0.119 = 10 \Rightarrow d = 84 \text{ km}"),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Mixing up focus and epicentre", "The **focus** is underground where rock breaks. The **epicentre** is on the surface above it."),
            MISTAKE("Saying Lake Nyos erupted lava", "The Nyos and Monoun disasters were **gas** releases (carbon dioxide) from lake water, not volcanic eruptions of lava or ash."),
            QUIZ("What killed people at Lake Nyos in 1986?", ["Lava flows", "A tsunami", "A cloud of carbon dioxide gas", "An earthquake"], 2, "A large cloud of carbon dioxide released from the lake suffocated people.", hint="The cloud was invisible and heavier than air.", feedback=["There was no lava flow at Nyos.", "A tsunami is a sea wave.", "", "The disaster was not caused by an earthquake."]),
            QUIZ("Which step reduces landslide risk on a steep slope?", ["Removing the trees", "Building a house at the top of the slope", "Planting trees and building drains", "Cutting into the base of the slope"], 2, "Roots and drains make the slope stronger and drier.", hint="Which option strengthens the slope?", feedback=["Removing trees weakens the soil.", "Adding weight makes things worse.", "", "Cutting the base steepens the slope."]),
            TIP("Geohazards and the Lake Nyos and Lake Monoun disasters appear in Paper 1 and Paper 2. Give the dates, the gas (carbon dioxide), why it was deadly (heavier than air, no smell) and how risk is reduced.", title="Exam link"),
            PQ("Explain what caused the 1986 disaster at Lake Nyos.", "Carbon dioxide of volcanic origin had built up in the deep water of the lake. A trigger made the water mix and the gas was suddenly released as a cloud. The gas is heavier than air and flowed down valleys, suffocating people and animals."),
            PQ("Name three volcanic hazards and one way to reduce the risk from volcanoes.", "Lava flows, ash fall and pyroclastic flows (or lahars, or gases). Risk is reduced by monitoring, warning systems and evacuation plans."),
            PQ("An earthquake is recorded by a seismograph with an S-P lag of 20 s. P travels at 6.0 km/s and S at 3.5 km/s. Find the distance to the epicentre.", "d = 20 x (6.0 x 3.5 / (6.0 - 3.5)) = 20 x 8.4 = 168 km (invented values for practice)."),
            SUM("A hazard becomes a risk when people and property are exposed and vulnerable.",
                "Earthquakes: focus underground, epicentre above; harm comes from shaking and its effects.",
                "Volcanic hazards include lava, ash, pyroclastic flows, lahars and gases. Mount Cameroon is active.",
                "Lake Monoun (1984) and Lake Nyos (1986): carbon dioxide gas releases; degassing pipes and planning reduce the risk."),
        ],
    ),
    lesson(
        module=M7, topic="Paper 1",
        title="Paper 1: multiple-choice strategies for Geology",
        slug="gly2-exam-paper-1-multiple-choice-strategies",
        minutes=22,
        summary="Paper 1 has 50 multiple-choice questions on the whole Geology syllabus. This lesson gives a two-pass time plan, elimination, a list of the precise definitions that cost marks, and quick checks for half-life, porosity and true-thickness questions.",
        objectives=[
            "Describe the structure of Paper 1: 50 multiple-choice questions on the whole syllabus",
            "Use a two-pass method and a time plan",
            "Use elimination and precise definitions to choose the best option",
            "Work quickly through half-life, porosity, true-thickness and scale questions",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Paper 1", "Two-pass method", "Elimination", "Precise definitions", "Calculation checks"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("Imagine you walk into the examination hall with a pen, a pencil, a ruler and a calculator that the Board allows. The paper has 50 questions. Some ask for a definition, some show a diagram of a fold or a fossil and some need a short calculation. A student who knows the content but not the method can still lose marks. This lesson is about method."),
            D("According to the GCE Board's 2023 report, Paper 1 of Geology (0555) is composed of **50 multiple-choice questions** to be **all answered**. The syllabus coverage of both papers was reported as 100%, so any topic from the course can appear. Check the exact time and mark rules with your teacher. In KKEVO's practice mock, Paper 1 is set at 90 minutes, which is a KKEVO practice setting.", title="Paper 1: the facts"),
            TIP("Paper 1 can test every module: global tectonics, minerals and crystals, rocks, surface processes, structure and stratigraphy, and resources and hazards. Ask your teacher for the weighting of each module.", title="Exam link"),
            H("Time: the two-pass method"),
            T("Fifty questions in 90 minutes is **1.8 minutes** a question. Do not spend five minutes on one question while easy ones wait."),
            KEY("A plan for the practice paper", "This is KKEVO study advice, not an official rule.", cards=[
                ["Pass 1 (about 55 minutes)", "Answer every question that you can do at once. Tick the others and move on."],
                ["Pass 2 (about 25 minutes)", "Return to the ticked questions. Use elimination."],
                ["Check (about 10 minutes)", "Make sure that every question has one answer. Re-check questions with 'not', 'except' and calculations."]]),
            H("Elimination and key words"),
            D("The **stem** is the question. The **key** is the correct option. The **distractors** are the wrong ones. Distractors are often true statements about something else, or common mistakes, so read all four options before you choose.", title="Stem, key and distractors"),
            KEY("Words to watch", "A single word can change the answer.", cards=[
                ["NOT or EXCEPT", "Find the option that is false. Cross out the three true ones."],
                ["Most, least, best", "More than one option may be partly true. Pick the best."],
                ["Always, never, only", "Rarely correct in geology, because there are exceptions."],
                ["Units", "Check units such as cm, km, m3, years and millions of years."]]),
            H("Definitions that earn marks"),
            T("The Board's report shows that candidates lose marks by giving a vague or wrong version of a basic definition. These are the ones to learn exactly. Hydrogeology is the study of **groundwater**, not just water. **Streak** is the **colour of the powder** of a mineral. **Fracture** is the **nature of a freshly broken surface**. **Lustre** is the **appearance of a mineral in reflected light**. A **plane** of symmetry is a **surface** that divides a crystal into two mirror-image halves; an **axis** of symmetry is an imaginary **line** about which a crystal is rotated through 360 degrees and a face appears more than once. A **horst** is a block pushed up between two faults; a **graben** is a block that has dropped. **Graded bedding** is a gradual change in grain size, coarse at the bottom and finer towards the top. **Ripple marks** are undulations made by moving fluid on sediment."),
            H("Quick checks for calculations"),
            KEY("Four calculation types", "Practise each until it takes less than two minutes.", cards=[
                ["Half-life", "Fraction left = (1/2) to the power n. Age = n x half-life."],
                ["Porosity", "Pore volume / total volume x 100."],
                ["True thickness", "Outcrop width x sin(dip), on flat ground."],
                ["Map scale", "Convert 'x cm = y km' to 1 : (y x 100 000 / x)."]]),
            EX("Worked example 1: elimination", intro="Which of these is NOT a type of unconformity? (A) Angular (B) Parallel (C) Heterolithic (D) Graded", steps=[
                S("Recall the types", text="Angular, parallel and heterolithic are all unconformities."),
                S("Check the odd one out", text="Graded is a kind of bedding (graded bedding), not an unconformity."),
                S("Answer", text="D."),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: a scale question", intro="A map shows a scale bar on which 4 cm represents 10 km. What is the numerical scale?", steps=[
                S("Convert 10 km to cm", math=r"10 \times 1000 \times 100 = 1\,000\,000 \text{ cm}"),
                S("Divide by the map distance", math=r"\frac{1\,000\,000}{4} = 250\,000"),
                S("Answer", text="The scale is 1 : 250 000."),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Spending too long on one hard question", "One question is worth 1 mark, like the easy ones. Tick it, move on and return in Pass 2."),
            MISTAKE("Leaving blanks", "Check the instructions on your own paper, but do not leave an answer blank when time runs out."),
            QUIZ("A question asks which option is NOT a physical property of minerals. Options: streak, lustre, cleavage, oxidation. What should you do?", ["Choose the first true property", "Find the odd one out", "Choose any option", "Skip the paper"], 1, "NOT means find the false option; oxidation is a weathering process, not a property of minerals.", hint="Cross out the three that are properties.", feedback=["The three properties are all true, so none of them is the answer.", "", "Guessing without reading is risky.", "You must answer all 50 questions."]),
            QUIZ("A sample has 12.5% of its parent isotope left and the half-life is 100 million years. How old is it?", ["100 million years", "200 million years", "300 million years", "800 million years"], 2, "12.5% = 1/8 = (1/2) to the power 3, so 3 half-lives = 300 million years.", hint="How many times must you halve 100% to get 12.5%?", feedback=["That is one half-life.", "That is two half-lives.", "", "That multiplies by 8 instead of 3."]),
            PQ("List the three steps of the two-pass method.", "Pass 1: answer the easy questions and tick the others. Pass 2: return to the ticked ones and use elimination. Final check: make sure that every question is answered and re-read those with NOT or EXCEPT."),
            PQ("Convert a graphical scale on which 5 cm represents 20 km into a numerical scale.", "20 km = 2 000 000 cm. 2 000 000 / 5 = 400 000, so the scale is 1 : 400 000."),
            PQ("Write precise definitions of streak and fracture.", "Streak is the colour of the powder of a mineral. Fracture is the nature of the surface of a freshly broken mineral."),
            SUM("Paper 1 has 50 multiple-choice questions to be all answered, covering the whole syllabus.",
                "Use two passes, about 1.8 minutes per question, and a final check.",
                "Eliminate distractors and watch NOT, EXCEPT, always and only.",
                "Learn precise definitions and practise the four quick calculations."),
        ],
    ),
    lesson(
        module=M7, topic="Paper 2",
        title="Paper 2: structured questions, diagrams and map interpretation",
        slug="gly2-exam-paper-2-structured-questions-maps",
        minutes=26,
        summary="Paper 2 has seven structural questions: answer two from Section A, two from Section B and the compulsory school-based question 7. This lesson shows how to choose questions, write exact definitions, draw diagrams and handle map work: scale, horizontal beds and cross-section profiles.",
        objectives=[
            "Describe the structure of Paper 2 and the choice rule",
            "Write short, exact answers to define-and-describe questions",
            "Convert a graphical scale to a numerical scale and find a gradient and vertical exaggeration",
            "Follow the steps for drawing a profile along a line of section on a geological map",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Paper 2", "Section A and B", "School-based assessment", "Map scale", "Line of section", "Profile", "Vertical exaggeration"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("Paper 1 asks you to choose an answer. Paper 2 asks you to **write** and to **draw**. Examiners reported that candidates were weak on three things: giving exact definitions, drawing clear diagrams and working with a geological map. Those are skills, and skills improve with practice."),
            D("According to the GCE Board's 2023 report, Paper 2 of Geology (0555) has **seven structural questions**. A candidate answers **five**: **two from Section A**, **two from Section B** and **Question 7**, which is a **school-based assessment** question and is **compulsory**. Check the exact time and marks with your teacher.", title="Paper 2: the facts"),
            KEY("The questions in 2023 covered a wide range", "The examiners' comments show the kind of material that appears.", cards=[
                ["Definitions", "Hydrogeology, types of unconformity, horst and graben, gangue, ore mineral and tenor, streak, fracture, lustre, plane and axis of symmetry."],
                ["Processes", "Graded bedding, ripple marks, hydration, carbonation and oxidation."],
                ["Fossils", "Characteristics of bivalves."],
                ["Map work", "Relationship between beds, profile along a line of section and conversion of the scale."]]),
            TIP("Because you must choose only two questions from each of Sections A and B, read all of them first and choose the ones where you can give exact definitions and clear diagrams. Question 7 is compulsory, so prepare for it from the beginning of the course.", title="Exam link"),
            H("Question 7: school-based assessment"),
            T("Question 7 is the school-based assessment question. Schools may set it differently, and it may be based on practical or project work done during the year. The report says teachers should take continuous assessment seriously. **Ask your teacher** what Question 7 looks like in your school, what you must keep (such as practical notes, field notes, sketches and map work) and how it is assessed. Keep every practical record safe."),
            H("Writing the answers"),
            KEY("Rules for structured answers", "Short, exact and labelled.", cards=[
                ["Define exactly", "Use the key words of the definition. 'Hydrogeology is the study of groundwater' scores; 'the study of water' does not."],
                ["Answer what is asked", "'State' needs a short fact. 'Describe' needs a short account. 'Explain' needs the reason."],
                ["Draw to help", "Use a pencil and a ruler. Label with lines that touch the part. Give the diagram a title."],
                ["Use examples", "A fossil name, a rock name or a Cameroonian example if you are sure of it."]]),
            H("Map work"),
            T("The examiners reported three difficulties on the map question: relating the beds to the topography, drawing the profile, and converting the scale. Here are the methods."),
            KEY("Relationship of beds and contours", "A key idea.", cards=[
                ["Boundaries parallel to the contours", "If the boundaries between beds run parallel to the contour lines, the beds are **horizontal**."],
                ["Boundaries that cross the contours", "Beds are **dipping** or folded or faulted. Use the dip symbol and the V shapes where boundaries cross valleys."],
                ["Strike and dip", "Read the strike line and dip symbol as in the lesson on strike and dip."]]),
            D("The **line of section** is the line on the map along which you draw the profile, for example A to B. A **profile** (cross-section) is a side view of the ground and the rocks beneath it along that line. It is **not** drawn as a flat line: the surface must follow the heights along the line.", title="Line of section and profile"),
            KEY("Steps for drawing a profile", "Work carefully.", cards=[
                ["1", "Lay the edge of a strip of paper along the line A to B. Mark A, B and every place where a contour or a bed boundary crosses the edge, and write the height or the bed name at each mark."],
                ["2", "Draw a horizontal line the same length as A to B. Mark a vertical scale for heights."],
                ["3", "Move the strip to the horizontal line. Plot a dot at each mark at its height, and join the dots in a smooth line. This is the ground surface."],
                ["4", "Mark where each bed boundary meets the surface. Using the dip (or horizontal), draw the boundaries downward. Shade or label each bed. Add a title, scales and labels at A and B."]]),
            F(r"\text{vertical exaggeration} = \frac{\text{horizontal scale denominator}}{\text{vertical scale denominator}}", title="Vertical exaggeration"),
            EX("Worked example 1: the scale", intro="On a map, 4 cm on the scale bar represents 10 km. Convert it to a numerical scale. The distance from A to B on the map is 6 cm. What is the real distance?", steps=[
                S("10 km in cm", math=r"10 \times 100\,000 = 1\,000\,000 \text{ cm}"),
                S("Numerical scale", math=r"1\,000\,000 \div 4 = 250\,000 \Rightarrow 1 : 250\,000"),
                S("Real distance", math=r"6 \times 250\,000 = 1\,500\,000 \text{ cm} = 15 \text{ km}"),
            ], check=r"6 \text{ cm} \times \frac{10 \text{ km}}{4 \text{ cm}} = 15 \text{ km}"),
            EX("Worked example 2: gradient and exaggeration", intro="The horizontal scale of a profile is 1 : 50 000. The vertical scale is 1 cm : 100 m, which is 1 : 10 000. (a) What is the vertical exaggeration? (b) Between two points on the line, the ground rises 120 m over 2.4 km. What is the gradient?", steps=[
                S("(a) Vertical exaggeration", math=r"\frac{50\,000}{10\,000} = 5"),
                S("(b) Gradient", math=r"2.4 \text{ km} = 2400 \text{ m}; \quad \frac{2400}{120} = 20"),
                S("Answer", text="The profile is exaggerated 5 times and the gradient is 1 in 20."),
            ]),
            MISTAKE("Drawing the profile as if the ground were flat", "The examiners reported that candidates drew a cross-section with a horizontal ground surface. The surface must follow the heights along the line of section."),
            MISTAKE("Giving the wrong form of definition", "Streak is the **colour** of a powder, not the powder itself. A plane of symmetry is a **surface**, and an axis is an imaginary **line**."),
            QUIZ("On a map, all the bed boundaries run parallel to the contour lines. What does this show about the beds?", ["They are vertical", "They are horizontal", "They are folded into a syncline", "They are faulted"], 1, "Boundaries that follow contours show beds at the same height everywhere, so they are horizontal.", hint="A contour joins points of equal height.", feedback=["Vertical beds give straight boundaries that cut across contours.", "", "A syncline shows the beds dipping in towards a central line.", "Faults show as breaks or offsets in the boundary lines."]),
            QUIZ("How many questions must a candidate answer in Paper 2 according to the Board's 2023 report?", ["Seven", "Five", "Four", "Three"], 1, "Five: two from Section A, two from Section B and compulsory Question 7.", hint="Add 2 + 2 + 1.", feedback=["There are seven questions in all but not all must be answered.", "", "Two plus two is four, but there is one more question.", "That is too few."]),
            PQ("State the rule for choosing questions in Paper 2.", "Answer five questions in all: two chosen from Section A, two chosen from Section B and the compulsory school-based question 7."),
            PQ("Convert a graphical scale of 5 cm = 20 km into a numerical scale and find the real distance for 7 cm on the map.", "20 km = 2 000 000 cm. 2 000 000 / 5 = 400 000, so the scale is 1 : 400 000. 7 cm represents 7 x 400 000 = 2 800 000 cm = 28 km."),
            PQ("List the steps for drawing a profile along a line A to B.", "Mark A, B and the crossing points of contours and bed boundaries on a strip of paper. Draw a baseline of the same length with a vertical scale. Plot the heights at the marks and join them for the ground surface. Add the bed boundaries using the dip, label the beds and add a title and scales."),
            SUM("Paper 2 has seven structural questions: two from Section A, two from Section B and compulsory Question 7 (school-based assessment).",
                "Write exact definitions and draw labelled diagrams in pencil.",
                "Beds that run parallel to contours are horizontal.",
                "Draw a profile that follows the surface heights, and convert scales carefully."),
        ],
    ),
]
